window.PREP_BANK=[{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which statement best describes bonding?","o":["Connecting a circuit conductor to earth only through a ground rod","Connecting conductive parts together to establish electrical continuity and conductivity","Identifying the grounded conductor with white insulation","Adding impedance to limit the available fault current"],"a":1,"e":"Bonded (bonding) is defined as connected to establish electrical continuity and conductivity. Bonding is what creates the metal path that carries fault current."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"What is the purpose of an effective ground-fault current path?","o":["To carry fault current into the earth, where it dissipates harmlessly","To reduce voltage drop on the neutral under normal load","To limit lightning voltage on the service drop","To carry fault current back to the source with low impedance so the overcurrent device or ground-fault detector operates"],"a":3,"e":"An effective ground-fault current path is a low-impedance path from the point of the fault to the source that facilitates operation of the overcurrent device or ground-fault detector."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A 120 V ungrounded conductor contacts a metal enclosure whose only connection is to a ground rod with 25 ohms resistance to earth. About how much fault current flows, and what happens to a 20 A breaker?","o":["About 4.8 A; the breaker does not open and the enclosure stays energized","About 48 A; the breaker opens in a few seconds","About 480 A; the breaker opens instantly","About 3,000 A; the breaker opens instantly"],"a":0,"e":"By Ohm’s law, 120 V / 25 ohms = 4.8 A. That is far below the 20 A rating, so the breaker will not open. This is why earth cannot serve as the ground-fault current path."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which statement about the earth and fault current is correct under 250.4(A)(5)?","o":["The earth may serve as the sole fault path if the ground rod is 25 ohms or less","The earth is the preferred return path for fault current","The earth shall not be considered an effective ground-fault current path","The earth may replace the equipment grounding conductor on equipment fastened in place"],"a":2,"e":"250.4(A)(5) states that the earth shall not be considered an effective ground-fault current path. The 25 ohm figure relates to supplementing single electrodes, not to clearing faults."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which system is required by 250.20(B) to be grounded?","o":["A three-wire, 480 V ungrounded delta system with ground detectors","A 208Y/120 V three-phase, four-wire wye system in which the neutral is used as a circuit conductor","An isolated power system supplying a hospital operating room","Any system supplied by a transformer, regardless of configuration"],"a":1,"e":"Three-phase, four-wire wye systems in which the neutral is a circuit conductor must be grounded. Certain systems, such as ungrounded delta systems meeting the conditions of 250.21 and isolated power systems, are permitted to be ungrounded."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"On a 240/120 V, three-phase, four-wire delta system with the center of one winding grounded, what is the voltage from the high-leg conductor to the grounded conductor, and how is it identified?","o":["120 V; identified by the color red","240 V; identified by the color white","277 V; identified by the color gray","208 V; identified by the color orange or other effective means"],"a":3,"e":"The high leg measures 208 V to the grounded conductor and must be identified by orange or another effective means (110.15). It must not be used to supply 120 V loads."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"According to 250.50, the grounding electrode system consists of:","o":["All electrodes described in 250.52(A)(1) through (A)(7) that are present at the building or structure, bonded together","Only the electrode closest to the service panel","Only a driven ground rod, regardless of other electrodes present","Whichever single electrode the installer prefers"],"a":0,"e":"250.50 requires all of the listed electrodes that are present at each building or structure served to be bonded together to form the grounding electrode system."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which of the following may NOT be used as a grounding electrode?","o":["A metal underground water pipe in contact with earth for 10 ft or more, supplemented by another electrode","A concrete-encased electrode","A metal underground gas piping system","A ground ring"],"a":2,"e":"250.52(B) lists metal underground gas piping systems and aluminum as not permitted for use as grounding electrodes."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which of the following satisfies the concrete-encased electrode requirement of 250.52(A)(3)?","o":["At least 10 ft of 3/8 in. reinforcing bar encased in 1 in. of concrete","At least 20 ft of 1/2 in. steel reinforcing bar or 20 ft of bare 4 AWG copper, encased in at least 2 in. of concrete in a foundation or footing in direct contact with earth","At least 8 ft of any metal pipe laid on top of a slab","At least 4 ft of 1/2 in. reinforcing bar in a basement wall above grade"],"a":1,"e":"A concrete-encased electrode uses at least 20 ft of 1/2 in. or larger rebar (or 20 ft of bare copper 4 AWG or larger) with at least 2 in. of concrete, located in a foundation or footing in direct contact with the earth."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"What minimum length of a rod electrode must be in contact with the soil?","o":["4 ft","6 ft","10 ft","8 ft"],"a":3,"e":"Rod and pipe electrodes must be at least 8 ft in length in contact with the soil (250.52(A)(5))."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A single driven rod is the only electrode and it measures 40 ohms to earth. What does 250.53 require?","o":["Supplement it with an additional electrode, spaced at least 6 ft from the first","Nothing; a single rod is always acceptable","Add a second rod placed within 3 ft of the first","Bond the rod to the metal gas piping"],"a":0,"e":"A single rod, pipe, or plate that does not have 25 ohms or less resistance to ground must be supplemented by an additional electrode, and additional rods must be at least 6 ft apart. Gas piping may not be used as an electrode."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"On a typical 120/240 V single-family service, where is the grounded conductor (neutral) bonded to the equipment grounding system?","o":["At every subpanel","At every receptacle","At the service, through the main bonding jumper","At the load end of each branch circuit"],"a":2,"e":"The neutral-to-ground bond is made at the service (main bonding jumper). Downstream, the neutral and equipment grounding conductors must be kept separate."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which marking satisfies the identification requirement for a grounded conductor of 6 AWG or smaller?","o":["A continuous green outer finish","A continuous white or gray outer finish, or three continuous white stripes on other than green insulation","A continuous red outer finish","Bare copper only"],"a":1,"e":"200.6 requires a grounded conductor to be identified by a continuous white or gray outer finish or by three continuous white stripes along its length on other than green insulation."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Who is responsible for interpreting the rules of the NEC on a particular installation?","o":["The equipment manufacturer","The lowest bidder’s project manager","The utility’s line crew","The authority having jurisdiction (AHJ)"],"a":3,"e":"Section 90.4 gives the authority having jurisdiction responsibility for enforcement and for interpretation of the Code rules, subject to the rules of the jurisdiction."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"What is the function of a grounding electrode conductor (GEC)?","o":["It connects the system grounded conductor or equipment to the grounding electrode system","It carries ground-fault current from branch-circuit equipment back to the panel","It identifies the neutral conductor","It serves as the main bonding jumper in a subpanel"],"a":0,"e":"The GEC connects the grounded conductor (or equipment) to the grounding electrode or to a point on the grounding electrode system."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"On a three-phase, three-wire corner-grounded delta system, the grounded conductor is:","o":["A neutral conductor","An equipment grounding conductor","A phase conductor, not a neutral","A main bonding jumper"],"a":2,"e":"A corner-grounded delta has no neutral point; the intentionally grounded conductor is one of the phase conductors. This is why “grounded conductor” and “neutral” are not synonyms."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which statement best distinguishes grounding from bonding?","o":["Grounding joins metal parts together; bonding connects the system to earth","Grounding connects the system to earth to limit voltage from lightning, surges, and contact with higher-voltage lines; bonding joins metal parts and the source to create a low-impedance fault path","They are identical terms for the same connection","Grounding applies only outdoors; bonding applies only indoors"],"a":1,"e":"System grounding addresses voltage control relative to earth, while bonding creates the continuous metal path back to the source needed to clear ground faults."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which of the following is a purpose of connecting an electrical system to earth, as stated in 250.4(A)(1)?","o":["To clear a line-to-enclosure fault by opening the overcurrent device","To allow the neutral to carry current","To eliminate the need for equipment grounding conductors","To limit voltage imposed by lightning, line surges, or unintentional contact with higher-voltage lines and to stabilize voltage to earth during normal operation"],"a":3,"e":"System grounding to earth is for voltage limiting and stabilization. Clearing faults is the job of the bonded effective ground-fault current path, not the earth connection."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Table 250.122 selects the minimum EGC size based on:","o":["The calculated load on the circuit","The ampacity of the circuit conductors after derating","The rating or setting of the automatic overcurrent device ahead of the circuit or equipment","The length of the circuit alone"],"a":2,"e":"Table 250.122 uses the rating or setting of the overcurrent device in the circuit ahead of the equipment, not the load or the conductor ampacity."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"What is the minimum copper EGC for a 20 A branch circuit?","o":["12 AWG","14 AWG","10 AWG","8 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The 20 A row of Table 250.122 calls for a 12 AWG copper EGC (10 AWG aluminum)."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"What is the minimum copper EGC for a feeder protected by a 60 A circuit breaker?","o":["12 AWG","8 AWG","6 AWG","10 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"The 60 A row of Table 250.122 gives 10 AWG copper (8 AWG aluminum)."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"What is the minimum copper EGC for a feeder protected by a 200 A circuit breaker?","o":["8 AWG","6 AWG","4 AWG","3 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"The 200 A row of Table 250.122 gives 6 AWG copper (4 AWG aluminum)."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"What is the minimum copper EGC for a feeder protected by a 400 A circuit breaker?","o":["4 AWG","2 AWG","3 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"The 400 A row of Table 250.122 gives 3 AWG copper (1 AWG aluminum)."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"What is the minimum aluminum EGC for a feeder protected by a 100 A circuit breaker?","o":["6 AWG","8 AWG","4 AWG","10 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The 100 A row of Table 250.122 gives 8 AWG copper and 6 AWG aluminum."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Table 250.122 gives an EGC size larger than the ungrounded circuit conductors that it accompanies. What is the general rule?","o":["The EGC must always be the size shown in the table","The EGC must be one size larger than the circuit conductors","The EGC is never smaller than the neutral","The EGC is not required to be larger than the ungrounded circuit conductors supplying the equipment"],"a":3,"e":"250.122(A) states that the EGC is not required to be larger than the circuit conductors supplying the equipment."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A 20 A circuit is run in 10 AWG instead of the minimum 12 AWG to limit voltage drop. What must be done with the wire-type EGC?","o":["Leave it at 12 AWG","Increase it in size proportionally, by circular mil area, with the ungrounded conductors","Replace it with the neutral","Remove it because the circuit is oversized"],"a":1,"e":"250.122(B) requires wire-type EGCs to be increased in size proportionally according to the circular mil area of the ungrounded conductors when those are upsized. Here the result is 10 AWG copper."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Several circuits share one raceway and one EGC. How is the EGC sized?","o":["For the smallest overcurrent device in the raceway","For the sum of all overcurrent devices","For the largest overcurrent device protecting conductors in the raceway","For the total connected load"],"a":2,"e":"250.122(C) allows one EGC for multiple circuits in the same raceway, sized from Table 250.122 for the largest overcurrent device protecting conductors in that raceway."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"An 800 A feeder uses parallel conductors installed in two raceways. What EGC is required?","o":["A 1/0 AWG copper EGC in each raceway, sized for the 800 A device","A 1/0 AWG copper EGC in only one of the raceways","A 3 AWG copper EGC in each raceway, sized for half the load","A 4/0 AWG copper EGC in each raceway"],"a":0,"e":"For conductors in parallel in multiple raceways, each raceway has an EGC sized from Table 250.122 based on the overcurrent device rating protecting the feeder. 800 A calls for 1/0 AWG copper."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"How must an insulated 6 AWG equipment grounding conductor be identified?","o":["With a continuous white outer finish","With a continuous gray outer finish","With red tape at each end","With a continuous outer finish that is green or green with one or more yellow stripes"],"a":3,"e":"Individually covered or insulated EGCs have a continuous outer finish that is green or green with one or more yellow stripes. White and gray are reserved for grounded conductors (200.6)."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which fastener is NOT permitted to connect a grounding conductor or connection device to an enclosure?","o":["A machine screw engaging at least two threads","A sheet-metal screw","A listed pressure connector","A thread-forming machine screw"],"a":1,"e":"250.8(B) prohibits sheet-metal screws for connecting grounding conductors or connection devices to enclosures. The other methods are permitted by 250.8(A)."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Outdoors, aluminum or copper-clad aluminum EGCs may not be terminated within what distance of the earth?","o":["3 in.","6 in.","18 in.","36 in."],"a":2,"e":"250.120(B) prohibits terminating aluminum or copper-clad aluminum EGCs within 18 in. of the earth outdoors, and prohibits direct contact with masonry or earth."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which of the following is recognized as an equipment grounding conductor under 250.118 when properly installed with listed fittings?","o":["Electrical metallic tubing (EMT)","Rigid PVC conduit","Flexible metal conduit of any length with any overcurrent device","A water pipe used in place of a conductor"],"a":0,"e":"RMC, IMC, and EMT are recognized as EGCs. Nonmetallic conduit is not, and flexible metal conduit qualifies only under limited conditions."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"When flexible metal conduit (FMC) is used as the equipment grounding means under the conditions of 250.118, the total length in any ground-return path is limited to:","o":["3 ft","10 ft","25 ft","6 ft"],"a":3,"e":"FMC is recognized as an EGC only if the total length in any ground-return path does not exceed 6 ft (among other conditions). Longer runs require a wire-type EGC."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"An old two-wire receptacle outlet has no equipment grounding conductor in the box. Which replacement is permitted by 406.4(D)(2)?","o":["A grounding-type receptacle with its grounding terminal connected to the neutral","A GFCI-type receptacle marked “No Equipment Ground”","A grounding-type receptacle with no GFCI protection and no marking","Any receptacle after cutting off the plug’s grounding pin"],"a":1,"e":"406.4(D)(2) permits a nongrounding receptacle, a GFCI receptacle marked “No Equipment Ground,” or a grounding-type receptacle supplied through a GFCI and marked appropriately. Connecting the grounding terminal to the neutral is never permitted."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"How is an isolated ground receptacle identified?","o":["By a red face plate","By a green dot on the yoke","By an orange triangle on the face of the receptacle","By a blue body color"],"a":2,"e":"Isolated ground receptacles are identified by an orange triangle on the face (406.3(D)). The isolated EGC may pass through boxes and panelboards without connecting to the enclosure, as permitted by 250.146(D)."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Where is the main bonding jumper installed at a service?","o":["At every subpanel","At the load end of each branch circuit","At the utility transformer secondary only","Within the service disconnect enclosure, unspliced, connecting the grounded conductor to the equipment grounding system"],"a":3,"e":"250.24(B) requires an unspliced main bonding jumper to connect the equipment grounding conductor(s) and the service disconnect enclosure to the grounded conductor within the enclosure for each service disconnect."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"On what is the size of the grounding electrode conductor at a service based, according to Table 250.66?","o":["The ampere rating of the branch circuits","The size of the largest ungrounded service-entrance conductor (or equivalent area for parallel conductors)","The length of the GEC run","The size of the neutral conductor only"],"a":1,"e":"Table 250.66 sizes the GEC from the largest ungrounded service-entrance conductor or the equivalent area for parallel sets."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Per Table 250.66, what size copper GEC is required for a service with 4/0 AWG copper ungrounded conductors?","o":["4 AWG","1/0 AWG","2 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"4/0 AWG copper falls in the row “over 3/0 through 350 kcmil,” which calls for a 2 AWG copper GEC."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Per Table 250.66, what size copper GEC is required for a service with 500 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors?","o":["1/0 AWG","2 AWG","2/0 AWG","3/0 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"500 kcmil copper falls in the row “over 350 through 600 kcmil,” which calls for a 1/0 AWG copper GEC."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A service has 600 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors, and the only electrode is a set of driven ground rods. What is the largest copper GEC size required for the connection to the rods?","o":["1/0 AWG","2 AWG","4 AWG","6 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"Table 250.66 would call for 1/0 AWG, but 250.66(A) states that the portion of the GEC that is the sole connection to rod, pipe, or plate electrodes is not required to be larger than 6 AWG copper."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A service has 600 kcmil copper ungrounded conductors, and the GEC connects only to a concrete-encased electrode. What is the largest copper GEC size required for that connection?","o":["6 AWG","4 AWG","2 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"250.66(B) states that the portion of the GEC that is the sole connection to a concrete-encased electrode is not required to be larger than 4 AWG copper."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which method is permitted to splice a grounding electrode conductor?","o":["A twist-on wire connector","A split-bolt connector wrapped with tape","Irreversible compression-type connectors listed for grounding and bonding, or exothermic welding","Soldering alone"],"a":2,"e":"250.64(C) requires a GEC to be installed in one continuous length except for listed irreversible compression-type connectors, exothermic welding, and the other limited cases it names."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A GEC is installed in a ferrous metal conduit for physical protection. What does 250.64(E) require?","o":["The conduit must be electrically continuous and bonded to the GEC at both ends","The conduit must be bonded at one end only","The conduit must be isolated from the GEC","Nothing; ferrous conduit has no effect on the GEC"],"a":0,"e":"Ferrous metal enclosures around a GEC create a choke effect, so they must be electrically continuous from the point of attachment to the grounding electrode and bonded to the GEC at both ends."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A subpanel is supplied by a four-wire feeder from the main service panel. How should the neutral and equipment grounding conductors be connected in the subpanel?","o":["Neutral and EGC on the same bar bonded to the enclosure","Neutral bonded to the enclosure; EGC left unconnected","Both isolated from the enclosure","Neutral bar isolated from the enclosure; EGC bar bonded to the enclosure"],"a":3,"e":"The neutral-to-ground bond is made only at the service (or at a separately derived system). In a subpanel, the neutral is isolated and the EGC bar is bonded to the enclosure."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"What is a likely consequence of a second neutral-to-ground connection at a subpanel?","o":["The overcurrent devices will open every time the panel is energized","Neutral current flows on the equipment grounding conductors, conduit, and enclosures, creating shock hazards and interfering with ground-fault protection","The system voltage will double","There is no consequence if the connection is tight"],"a":1,"e":"A downstream neutral-to-ground bond creates parallel neutral paths. Normal neutral current flows on the EGC and metal parts, producing touch voltage and interfering with ground-fault protective equipment."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A new detached garage is supplied by a feeder from the house. What does 250.32 require for the feeder and neutral?","o":["Only three conductors, with the neutral bonded to the garage panel enclosure","A neutral only; no EGC is needed","An equipment grounding conductor run with the feeder, with the neutral isolated from the EGC at the garage panel, plus a grounding electrode at the garage","A ground rod at the garage, with the neutral and EGC bonded together at the garage panel"],"a":2,"e":"For new installations, an equipment grounding conductor is installed with the supply conductors to the detached building, the neutral is not connected to the EGC or ground at the building, and a grounding electrode is installed at the building (subject to the stated exception)."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which statement about locknuts and bushings at the service is correct?","o":["Standard locknuts or bushings may not be the sole means of bonding required at the service","A standard locknut inside and outside the box is always sufficient","Bonding is not required for service raceways","Paint on the enclosure improves bonding"],"a":0,"e":"250.92(B) provides that standard locknuts or bushings shall not be the sole means for the bonding required at the service. Bonding-type locknuts, bushings, bushings with jumpers, or other listed means are used."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"The additional bonding methods of 250.97 apply to metal raceways and cables containing circuits that operate at over:","o":["50 V to ground","150 V to ground","600 V phase-to-phase only","250 V to ground"],"a":3,"e":"250.97 applies to circuits of over 250 V to ground, which includes the 277 V circuits of a 480Y/277 V system but not the 120 V circuits of a 208Y/120 V system."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Interior metal water piping located more than what distance from the point of entrance to the building may not be used as part of the grounding electrode system or as a conductor to interconnect electrodes?","o":["2 ft","5 ft","10 ft","25 ft"],"a":1,"e":"250.68(C)(1) limits the use of interior metal water piping to the first 5 ft from the point of entrance for these purposes."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which statement about metal gas piping is correct?","o":["It may be used as the sole electrode if at least 10 ft is in the earth","It must be used as an electrode to satisfy 250.50","Underground metal gas piping may not be used as a grounding electrode, and gas piping capable of becoming energized is subject to the bonding requirements of 250.104(B)","Bonding of gas piping is never required"],"a":2,"e":"250.52(B) prohibits metal underground gas piping as a grounding electrode. Separately, 250.104(B) requires bonding of other metal piping, including gas piping, that is capable of becoming energized."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"At a separately derived system, where is the system bonding jumper installed?","o":["At a single point, either at the source or at the first system disconnecting means, but not both","At both the source and every downstream panel","At each branch circuit","Nowhere; the SDS neutral is never bonded"],"a":0,"e":"250.30(A) requires the system bonding jumper to be made at a single point from the source to the first disconnecting means, so that the neutral is bonded once, and the GEC connects at the same point."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A permanently installed generator is connected through a 4-pole transfer switch that switches the neutral. How is the generator treated?","o":["As a non-separately derived system, with no bonding at the generator","As a service, with a main bonding jumper at the transfer switch","As portable equipment exempt from bonding","As a separately derived system, with a system bonding jumper and grounding electrode connection per 250.30"],"a":3,"e":"When the neutral is switched, there is no direct connection between the normal and alternate source neutrals, so the generator is a separately derived system."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A generator is connected through a 3-pole transfer switch that does not switch the neutral. Where should the neutral-to-ground bond be?","o":["At the generator only","Only at the service; the generator neutral is not bonded to the generator frame","At both the generator and the service","At the transfer switch only, and not at the service"],"a":1,"e":"With a 3-pole transfer switch the generator is not separately derived. The neutral-to-ground bond stays at the service, and bonding the generator neutral to its frame would create a second bond and parallel neutral paths."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"An inspector finds the neutral and equipment grounding conductors terminated on the same bar, bonded to the enclosure, in a subpanel fed by a four-wire feeder. What correction is required?","o":["Isolate the neutral bar from the enclosure and land the EGCs on a bar bonded to the enclosure","Add a second ground rod at the subpanel","Replace the feeder with a larger conductor","No correction; this is acceptable at any panel"],"a":0,"e":"On the load side of the service, the neutral and EGC must be kept separate, with the EGC bar bonded to the enclosure and the neutral bar isolated."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A new detached workshop is fed from the house. Which feeder arrangement meets the Code for new work?","o":["Two ungrounded conductors and a neutral only, with the neutral bonded to the shop panel","A single neutral conductor and a ground rod","Two ungrounded conductors, a neutral, and an EGC, with the neutral isolated from the EGC at the shop panel and a grounding electrode at the shop","Two ungrounded conductors with the EGC omitted"],"a":2,"e":"250.32 requires an EGC to run with the supply conductors to a building supplied by a feeder, with the neutral kept separate and a grounding electrode system at the building."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which finding at a final inspection is a violation?","o":["Rebar, a water pipe, and a rod bonded together","A metal underground water pipe used as the only grounding electrode with no supplemental electrode","A listed exothermic weld on a GEC","A GEC sized from Table 250.66"],"a":1,"e":"A metal underground water pipe electrode must be supplemented by an additional electrode (250.53(D)(2)), and all electrodes present are bonded together (250.50)."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which test method is used to measure the resistance of a ground electrode to earth?","o":["Receptacle tester plugged into a nearby outlet","Continuity test between the neutral and the EGC","Voltage measurement between the two hot legs","Fall-of-potential test with an earth tester"],"a":3,"e":"Earth resistance of an electrode is measured with a fall-of-potential earth tester (or a clamp-on method on connected systems), per instrument instructions."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A contractor bonds the neutral of a generator to its frame, but the generator is connected through a 3-pole transfer switch. What is the problem?","o":["It creates a second neutral-to-ground bond and parallel neutral paths because the generator is not separately derived","Nothing; every generator must have its neutral bonded to the frame","The frame must be bonded to the utility neutral through the gas line","The transfer switch must be replaced by a 2-pole switch"],"a":0,"e":"With a 3-pole transfer switch the generator is not an SDS. The neutral-to-ground bond is at the service only, and a second bond at the generator causes objectionable current."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"An older house has boxes with no EGC. Which option is permitted by 406.4(D)(2) when replacing receptacles?","o":["A grounding-type receptacle with the grounding terminal connected to the neutral","A grounding-type receptacle with no GFCI and no marking","A GFCI receptacle marked “No Equipment Ground”","Cutting the grounding prong off the plug"],"a":2,"e":"406.4(D)(2) permits a nongrounding receptacle, a GFCI-type receptacle marked “No Equipment Ground,” or a grounding-type receptacle supplied through a GFCI and marked appropriately."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"What is the minimum copper EGC for a feeder protected by a 100 A breaker?","o":["10 AWG","8 AWG","6 AWG","4 AWG"],"a":1,"e":"Table 250.122 gives 8 AWG copper for a 100 A overcurrent device."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"You are installing a new 200 A service with 3/0 AWG copper ungrounded conductors. Using Table 250.66, what size copper GEC is called for?","o":["6 AWG","2 AWG","1/0 AWG","4 AWG"],"a":3,"e":"3/0 AWG copper is in the row “2/0 AWG or 3/0 AWG,” which calls for a 4 AWG copper GEC. If the GEC goes only to rods, it need not exceed 6 AWG copper."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"In an inspection of a concrete foundation before the pour, which item should be confirmed for the concrete-encased electrode?","o":["At least 20 ft of 1/2 in. rebar (or 20 ft of bare 4 AWG copper) in the footing, in contact with earth, with 2 in. of concrete cover, and an accessible connection point","At least 4 ft of 3/8 in. rebar in the wall above grade","That a 6 ft ground rod is leaning against the foundation","That the rebar has been painted"],"a":0,"e":"250.52(A)(3) defines the concrete-encased electrode. The connection point must be accessible after the pour so the GEC can be attached and the electrode bonded."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A contractor wants to use green 12 AWG insulated wire as a switch leg. Is this permitted?","o":["Yes, if the wire is labeled at both ends","Yes, if no other colors are available","No. Green or green-with-yellow-stripe insulation may not be used for ungrounded or grounded circuit conductors","Yes, in any circuit under 50 V"],"a":2,"e":"250.119 prohibits conductors with green or green-with-yellow-stripe insulation from being used as ungrounded or grounded circuit conductors."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"The high-leg conductor on a 240/120 V, three-phase, four-wire delta system must be identified by:","o":["The color green","The color orange or another effective means","The color white","The color gray"],"a":1,"e":"110.15 requires the high-leg conductor, which measures 208 V to ground, to be identified by orange or another effective means."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"For a 480Y/277 V transformer that is a separately derived system, where is the system bonding jumper installed?","o":["At the source and at every downstream panel","Only at the utility service","At each branch-circuit breaker","At a single point at the source or at the first system disconnecting means, not both, with the GEC connected at the same point"],"a":3,"e":"250.30(A) requires a single system bonding jumper connection, with the grounding electrode conductor connected at the same point."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"What is the minimum aluminum EGC for a feeder protected by a 400 A breaker?","o":["1 AWG","2 AWG","3 AWG","1/0 AWG"],"a":0,"e":"The 400 A row of Table 250.122 gives 3 AWG copper and 1 AWG aluminum."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A clamp-on ammeter around the EGC of a feeder reads several amperes under normal load. What is the most likely cause?","o":["The EGC is oversized","The feeder voltage is too high","Neutral current is returning on the EGC through an unintended neutral-to-ground connection","The overcurrent device is rated too high"],"a":2,"e":"An EGC should carry little or no current during normal operation. Measurable current under load indicates a neutral-to-ground connection downstream (or a miswire) that diverts neutral current onto the ground path."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Why are the grounding systems of different systems, such as communications and power, bonded together?","o":["To allow each system to carry the other’s load current","To limit potential differences between the systems’ metal parts and conductors","To eliminate the need for a grounding electrode","To raise the voltage to ground on the power system"],"a":1,"e":"Bonding the systems together reduces voltage differences between them, which reduces shock hazards and equipment damage. Verify the exact intersystem bonding requirements in the adopted edition."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"A new 4-wire branch circuit is run for an electric clothes dryer. How should the neutral and equipment grounding conductor be treated at the receptacle?","o":["Joined together at the receptacle, with the EGC left unconnected","Both connected to the neutral terminal","The EGC omitted since the neutral grounds the frame","Kept separate, with the equipment frame bonded to the EGC"],"a":3,"e":"For new circuits, ranges and dryers use a four-wire connection with a separate EGC (250.140), and the neutral is not used to ground the equipment frame."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"Which edition of the NEC governs your installation?","o":["The edition adopted by the jurisdiction (in Wisconsin, as adopted through the state rules and any local amendments), regardless of the newest edition published","Always the most recently published edition","Whichever edition the manufacturer references","Whichever edition the installer prefers"],"a":0,"e":"The jurisdiction determines which edition is legally in force, and the AHJ interprets the Code for the project."},{"c":"Grounding & bonding","q":"What is the minimum copper EGC for a feeder protected by a 1000 A device?","o":["1/0 AWG","3/0 AWG","2/0 AWG","4/0 AWG"],"a":2,"e":"The 1000 A row of Table 250.122 gives 2/0 AWG copper (4/0 AWG aluminum)."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The 2023 NEC revision cycle was primarily driven by growth in which three areas?","o":["Residential wiring, commercial lighting, and industrial motors","Electric vehicle charging, energy storage systems, and photovoltaic solar","Arc flash safety, grounding, and bonding","AFCI protection, GFCI protection, and surge protection"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC revision cycle focused on EV charging, energy storage systems, and PV solar — all three received new or significantly expanded articles including new Articles 626 and 691."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Which of the following is a brand new article added to the 2023 NEC that did not exist in the 2020 edition?","o":["Article 625 — EV charging","Article 691 — Large-scale PV electric supply stations","Article 690 — PV systems","Article 706 — Energy storage systems"],"a":1,"e":"Article 691 covering large-scale PV electric supply stations is brand new in 2023. Articles 625, 690, and 706 existed before but received major updates."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 230.67, what new device is now required at the service equipment of all dwelling units?","o":["A whole-house generator transfer switch","A listed surge protective device (SPD)","A main breaker with 200A minimum rating","A ground fault circuit interrupter for the main panel"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 230.67 requires a listed surge protective device (SPD) at the service equipment for all dwelling units. This is a new mandatory requirement — it was not in the 2020 NEC."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"In the 2023 NEC, the definition of 'Qualified Person' was updated to clarify that training must include:","o":["Only electrical theory — voltage, current, and resistance","Specific knowledge of the construction and operation of the equipment they will work on","Only OSHA safety regulations","NFPA 70E training for arc flash"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC updated the Qualified Person definition to clarify that training must include knowledge related to the specific equipment or installations they will work on or near — not just general electrical theory."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"New Article 626 was added to the 2023 NEC to cover:","o":["Residential EV charging in garages","Electrified truck parking spaces","Large-scale battery storage","Commercial EV fleet charging stations"],"a":1,"e":"Article 626 covers electrified truck parking spaces — EV charging infrastructure specifically for large trucks and commercial vehicles. This is separate from Article 625 which covers passenger vehicle EV charging."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"How often is the NEC revised?","o":["Every year","Every 2 years","Every 3 years","Every 5 years"],"a":2,"e":"The National Electrical Code (NFPA 70) is revised on a 3-year cycle. The most recent editions are 2017, 2020, and 2023. States adopt each edition on their own timeline, which may lag significantly behind the published edition."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"New Article 360 added to the 2023 NEC covers safety requirements for:","o":["Solar panel racking systems","Capacitors","Emergency disconnects","Electric vehicle batteries"],"a":1,"e":"New Article 360 in the 2023 NEC covers safety-related requirements for capacitors — including energy storage capacitors and power factor correction capacitors. Annex R on working with capacitors was also added."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per the 2023 NEC, shaded text and vertical bars in the margin of the code book indicate:","o":["Sections that have been deleted","New or revised requirements from the previous edition","Optional requirements that may be waived","Requirements that apply only in specific states"],"a":1,"e":"In the NEC code book, shaded text and vertical bars in the margin identify new or revised requirements compared to the previous edition. This is the standard NEC method for tracking changes across editions."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A journeyman electrician licensed in Wisconsin needs to know which NEC edition applies to their work. The best source is:","o":["The current edition on sale at the bookstore","The 2023 NEC — always the most current and binding","The Wisconsin DSPS website — states adopt independently","The local utility company's standards"],"a":2,"e":"States adopt the NEC independently and on different timelines. The Wisconsin DSPS (dsps.wi.gov) is the authoritative source for which edition is currently adopted in Wisconsin. Working to the wrong edition can result in inspection failures."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The 2023 NEC added bidirectional charging requirements to Article 625 to address:","o":["Standard EV charging from the grid to the vehicle","EV supply equipment that can also draw power from the vehicle back to the premises (V2H) or grid (V2G)","Two separate charging connectors on a single EV","Both Level 1 and Level 2 charging simultaneously"],"a":1,"e":"Bidirectional charging (V2H and V2G) allows EV supply equipment to both charge the vehicle from the grid and draw power from the vehicle battery back to the home or grid. The 2023 NEC added requirements for this new capability in Article 625."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Which new inspection checkpoint will appear in jurisdictions adopting 2023 NEC for clothes closet luminaires?","o":["All luminaires must have a vapor-tight lens","LED luminaires are now required — incandescent and fluorescent are no longer permitted","Luminaires must be surface-mounted only","A dedicated circuit is required for each closet luminaire"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 410.16 was completely revised to require LED luminaires in clothes closets, eliminating the prior allowance for incandescent and fluorescent sources due to heat proximity and fire hazard concerns."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Article 215 was updated in the 2023 NEC to require AFCI protection on:","o":["Only residential service feeders","Feeders serving dwelling unit branch circuits — a new 2023 requirement","All commercial feeders over 100 amperes","Any feeder with aluminum conductors"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC updated Article 215 to require AFCI protection on feeders serving dwelling unit branch circuits. This was not required in prior NEC editions and extends the AFCI protection requirements upstream to the feeder level."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The NFPA publishes which document that identifies every change between NEC editions?","o":["The NEC Handbook","The First Draft Report — available free at nfpa.org","The NFPA 70E supplement","The Illustrated Guide to the NEC"],"a":1,"e":"NFPA publishes the First Draft Report which documents every proposed and accepted change between NEC editions, including the technical basis for each change. It is available as a free download at nfpa.org."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Which statement is correct about 2023 NEC adoption?","o":["All states automatically adopt the 2023 NEC on its publication date","States adopt the NEC independently — some may be 1–5 years behind the published edition","Only states with populations over 1 million are required to adopt the 2023 NEC","The 2023 NEC applies only to commercial work — residential uses the 2020 NEC"],"a":1,"e":"States adopt the NEC independently and at different rates. Some states are still on the 2017 or 2020 edition. Electricians must know which edition applies in their state and verify with their state licensing board."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"New Article 691 in the 2023 NEC covers requirements for:","o":["Residential battery storage systems","Large-scale PV electric supply stations — utility-scale solar","EV fast-charging stations","Emergency power supply systems"],"a":1,"e":"Article 691 is brand new in the 2023 NEC and covers large-scale PV electric supply stations — utility-scale solar installations. This fills a gap in the prior code where utility-scale solar was not specifically addressed by the NEC."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"What is the primary purpose of the 2023 NEC 230.67 surge protective device requirement?","o":["To protect the utility transformer from lightning","To protect dwelling unit electrical equipment and appliances from voltage surges","To satisfy OSHA electrical safety requirements","To replace the need for individual outlet surge protectors"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 230.67 requires a listed SPD at the service equipment of dwelling units to protect the building's electrical equipment and appliances from transient voltage surges, which can damage sensitive electronics and shorten equipment life."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, the tamper-resistant receptacle requirements of 406.12 were expanded to include which new occupancy types?","o":["Warehouses and manufacturing facilities","Child care facilities, healthcare occupancies, hotels/motels, and preschools","Agricultural buildings and temporary construction sites","Industrial process control areas"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 406.12 expanded tamper-resistant receptacle requirements to child care facilities, healthcare occupancy patient care areas, hotels/motels, and preschool/elementary school classrooms — all new additions beyond the existing dwelling unit requirement."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"For an electrician transitioning from the 2020 NEC, the most practical first step when reviewing the 2023 code book is:","o":["Read every article from Article 100 to the appendices","Focus only on Article 250 grounding changes","Look for shaded text and vertical bar indicators to identify new and revised requirements","Memorize all new article numbers before reading content"],"a":2,"e":"The most efficient approach when transitioning between NEC editions is to use the code book's change indicators — shaded text and vertical bars in the margin identify exactly where changes were made. This lets you focus on what's new rather than re-reading unchanged requirements."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The 2023 NEC expanded AFCI protection under 210.12 to include which new occupancy type?","o":["Industrial facilities","Dormitories, hotel/motel guest rooms, and guest suites","Commercial office buildings","Agricultural buildings"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 210.12 expanded AFCI protection to dormitories, hotel/motel guest rooms, and guest suites — locations that were not required to have AFCI protection under the 2020 NEC."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Which new GFCI requirement was added under 2023 NEC 210.8 that was not in the 2020 edition?","o":["All outdoor receptacles in commercial buildings","Dishwasher branch circuits — 210.8(A)(11)","All receptacles within 10 feet of any water source","Kitchen receptacles regardless of distance from the sink"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 210.8(A)(11) added GFCI protection requirements for dishwasher branch circuits — a new addition in the 2023 edition that was not required in 2020."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 230.67(A), when must a surge protective device be installed at dwelling unit service equipment?","o":["Only when the homeowner requests it","When the service is new or replaced — mandatory at all dwelling unit services","Only when the service is over 200 amperes","Only in areas with frequent lightning strikes"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 230.67 requires a listed surge protective device at the service equipment for all new dwelling unit service installations. 230.67(A) also triggers the requirement when service equipment is replaced."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 250.94, an intersystem bonding termination is now required on:","o":["Only structures with communications systems already installed","All structures with AC electrical service — regardless of communications systems","Only commercial structures over 10,000 sq ft","Only structures with solar or EV charging"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 250.94 strengthened the requirement so that an intersystem bonding termination device is required on ALL structures with AC service — ensuring the termination point is available even if no communications system is currently installed."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"New to the 2023 NEC, feeder AFCI protection under Article 215.9 applies to feeders supplying:","o":["All feeders over 100 amperes","120V, 15A and 20A branch circuits to dwelling units where branch circuit AFCI is not installed","All residential feeders — both 120V and 240V","Only feeders from the utility to the main panel"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 215.9(A) requires AFCI protection on feeders supplying 120V, 15A and 20A branch circuits to dwelling units. An exception applies when listed AFCI circuit breakers are installed at the branch circuit level."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The 2023 NEC added which requirement for outdoor emergency disconnects for single-family dwellings?","o":["A separate meter socket outside the dwelling","An outside emergency disconnect per 225.41 — accessible to first responders","A generator interlock at the main panel","A listed SPD at the outdoor disconnect"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 225.41 requires that feeders supplying 1- and 2-family dwellings include an outside emergency disconnect that allows first responders to de-energize the dwelling from outside — important for fire and emergency response."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"When grounding a rooftop PV system under the 2023 NEC, an electrician must comply with:","o":["Article 250 only","Article 690 only","Both Article 250 AND Article 690 — new cross-references added in 2023","Article 690 for grounding, Article 706 for bonding"],"a":2,"e":"The 2023 NEC added explicit cross-references in Article 250 pointing to Articles 690 (PV) and 706 (ESS) for special grounding requirements. PV installations must comply with both the general Article 250 requirements AND the specific PV grounding provisions in Article 690."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 250.104(B), metal gas piping bonding conductors are sized using:","o":["Table 250.66 based on service conductor size","Table 250.122 based on the largest OCPD protecting the branch circuit nearest the piping","A minimum of 6 AWG copper regardless of circuit size","The same size as the EGC for the feeder"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC clarified that metal gas piping bonding conductors use Table 250.122, sized based on the largest OCPD protecting the branch circuit nearest the piping. This is different from water piping which uses Table 250.66."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The 2023 NEC intersystem bonding termination under 250.94 must be located:","o":["At the grounding electrode","Accessible, within 3 feet of the service entrance","In the main electrical panel only","At each communications system demarc"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 250.94 requires the intersystem bonding termination to be accessible and located within 3 feet of the service entrance. This placement makes it practical for communications system installers to connect their bonding conductors."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Which type of SPD is acceptable at the service equipment per 2023 NEC 230.67?","o":["Type 3 only — point-of-use SPD","Type 1 or Type 2 — listed for use at service equipment","Any commercially available surge strip","Only UL-listed SPDs above 40kA surge current rating"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 230.67 requires a listed Type 1 or Type 2 SPD at the service equipment. Type 3 SPDs are point-of-use devices (outlet protectors) and do not satisfy the service equipment requirement. The SPD must be listed and suitable for service equipment installation."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"What must an electrician do when replacing service equipment on a dwelling unit under the 2023 NEC?","o":["No additional requirements if the service was previously code-compliant","Install a listed surge protective device (SPD) at the new service equipment per 230.67(A)","Install a whole-house generator interlock","Only update the grounding electrode system if the existing one is defective"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 230.67(A) triggers the SPD requirement when service equipment is replaced — not just on new services. If you replace the service panel on an existing dwelling, an SPD must be installed."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Which statement about 2023 NEC AFCI requirements for dormitories is correct?","o":["AFCI is not required in dormitories — only dwelling units","AFCI is required in dormitories under 2023 NEC 210.12(B) — a new addition","AFCI is required only in dormitories over 50 units","AFCI is only required in dormitory kitchens and bathrooms"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 210.12(B) is a new addition that extends AFCI protection requirements to dormitories. This was not required in the 2020 NEC and represents a significant expansion of AFCI coverage into non-dwelling occupancies."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 250.94, the intersystem bonding termination device must be capable of accepting a minimum of:","o":["One conductor","Two conductors","Three conductors","Five conductors"],"a":2,"e":"2023 NEC 250.94 requires the intersystem bonding termination to be a listed device capable of accepting at least three conductors — to accommodate multiple communications systems (cable, telephone, and satellite/fiber) that may need to bond to the electrical system."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A 2023 NEC-compliant dishwasher installation requires:","o":["A dedicated 20A circuit only","A dedicated circuit with GFCI protection per 210.8(A)(11)","A 15A circuit is always acceptable for dishwashers","No special requirements — standard kitchen circuit is acceptable"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 210.8(A)(11) requires GFCI protection for dishwasher branch circuits — a new addition in the 2023 edition. This applies to dishwashers in all occupancy types, not just dwelling units."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, when must a concrete-encased electrode (Ufer ground) include 20 feet of rebar or wire?","o":["Only when soil resistivity exceeds 1000 Ω·m","Always — this is the minimum per 250.52(A)(3)","Only for commercial buildings — residential may use a shorter length","Only when other electrodes are not available"],"a":1,"e":"NEC 250.52(A)(3) requires a minimum of 20 feet of steel reinforcing bar (minimum #4) or bare copper conductor (minimum 4 AWG) for a concrete-encased electrode. This minimum is unchanged in 2023 but was clarified in its application."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"An electrician is installing a new subpanel in a dwelling unit addition. Under the 2023 NEC, the feeder to this subpanel:","o":["Has no AFCI requirements — only branch circuits need AFCI","May require AFCI protection per 215.9 if AFCI circuit breakers are not used at the branch circuit level","Requires AFCI only if the feeder is aluminum conductor","Requires AFCI protection only if the feeder is longer than 50 feet"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 215.9(A) requires AFCI protection on feeders supplying 120V, 15A and 20A dwelling unit branch circuits unless listed AFCI circuit breakers are installed at the branch circuit level. A new subpanel feeder in a dwelling unit addition must meet this requirement."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"What label is required on service equipment where an SPD is installed per 2023 NEC 230.67(B)?","o":["No label required if the SPD is visible","A label identifying the SPD installation — required by 230.67(B)","The SPD manufacturer's nameplate only","An arc flash hazard label per NEC 110.16"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 230.67(B) requires that service equipment where an SPD is installed be labeled to identify the presence of the SPD. This documentation requirement ensures future electricians and inspectors know an SPD has been installed."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The 2023 NEC update to Article 250 for PV systems primarily added:","o":["New grounding electrode requirements specific to PV","Cross-references to Article 690 for specific PV grounding requirements","A new table for PV system grounding conductor sizing","Elimination of grounding for transformerless PV inverters"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC added explicit cross-references in Article 250 pointing electricians to Article 690 for PV-specific grounding requirements. This helps ensure PV installers consult both the general grounding article and the PV-specific article when designing grounding systems."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 334.17, NM cable requires protection when it passes through wood framing within what distance of the edge?","o":["1 inch","1.25 inches","1.5 inches","2 inches"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 334.17 requires a steel plate or equivalent protection when NM cable passes through holes in wood framing within 1.25 inches of the edge — to prevent damage from drywall screws or other fasteners."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The 2023 NEC Article 310 updates to conductor ampacity focused primarily on:","o":["Eliminating aluminum conductors from residential use","Updating temperature correction factors and provisions for high-temperature environments","Reducing minimum wire sizes for branch circuits","Changing color coding requirements for conductors"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC Article 310 updates focused on temperature correction factors in 310.15(B) and provisions for high-temperature installations — reflecting modern applications in data centers, industrial processes, and other environments."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 334.30, NM cable must be secured within what distance of every box, conduit body, or fitting?","o":["6 inches","12 inches","18 inches","24 inches"],"a":1,"e":"NEC 334.30 requires NM cable to be secured within 12 inches of every box, conduit body, or fitting. The maximum interval between supports is 4.5 feet. These intervals were not changed in 2023 but are frequently cited on inspections."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Which article covers box fill calculations in the 2023 NEC?","o":["Article 300","Article 310","Article 314","Article 334"],"a":2,"e":"Article 314 covers outlet, device, pull, and junction boxes — including box fill calculations under 314.16. The 2023 NEC updated some volume values and clarified how certain fittings are counted."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 334.80, the ampacity of NM cable is limited by which temperature rating?","o":["75°C — the standard rating for NM cable","60°C — the termination temperature limitation for most NM cable","90°C — NM cable is now rated to 90°C in 2023","The temperature rating depends on the insulation color"],"a":1,"e":"NM cable ampacity is limited to 60°C per 334.80 (referencing 310.15), even though the conductor insulation itself may be rated to 90°C. This is because NM cable is typically installed through wood framing and in conditions that limit heat dissipation."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, when cable tray is used in a data center air-handling space, the cables must be:","o":["Any cable type is acceptable in cable tray","Listed for use in air-handling (plenum) spaces — per 392.18(H) and 300.22","Only metal-clad cable is permitted in cable tray in data centers","Individually enclosed in metal conduit within the cable tray"],"a":1,"e":"Per 2023 NEC 392.18(H) and 300.22, cables installed in cable tray in air-handling spaces must be listed for use in those spaces (plenum-rated). This was updated and clarified in 2023 for data center applications."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A journeyman electrician is installing a multi-wire branch circuit in a dwelling unit. Per 2023 NEC, how must the circuit be identified?","o":["With colored tape at each outlet only","At the panel — each wire identified by circuit or breaker handle tie — no change from 2020","No identification required if conductors are different colors","Multi-wire branch circuits are prohibited in dwelling units under 2023 NEC"],"a":1,"e":"Multi-wire branch circuits must be identified at the panel so that all conductors of a MWBC are identified. This allows safe servicing — all conductors of the MWBC must be simultaneously de-energized. This requirement is unchanged in 2023 but remains an important inspection point."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Underground conduit for EV charging circuits must meet burial depth requirements under which 2023 NEC section?","o":["Article 625 only — EV specific","300.5 — underground wiring burial depths (updated for EV-specific circuits in 2023)","230.67 — service equipment requirements","250.52 — grounding electrode burial requirements"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 300.5 governs underground wiring burial depths. The 2023 edition added clarifications for EV charging circuits and PV system underground wiring, which may have different burial depth requirements than standard branch circuits."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 392.22, cable tray conductor fill rules were updated to address:","o":["Only single conductor cable fills","Larger conductor combinations — particularly for high-ampacity power distribution systems","Low-voltage signal cable fill in power cable tray","Maximum number of conductors regardless of size"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 392.22 updates to cable tray fill address larger conductor combinations, particularly relevant for data center and industrial power distribution where multiple large conductors may share a cable tray system."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Which conductor color is required for the high-leg of a 208Y/120V or 240/120V delta system per the NEC?","o":["Red","Blue","Orange","Green with yellow stripe"],"a":2,"e":"Per NEC 230.56 and 408.3(F), the high-leg of a delta system (208V or 120V above neutral) must be identified with an orange color or marking. This is a consistent NEC requirement that warns electricians of the higher voltage to neutral on this leg."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"NM cable in thermal insulation (per 334.23) is subject to what special requirement in the 2023 NEC?","o":["NM cable cannot be used in insulated walls","NM cable must be derated when surrounded by insulation — updated clearance requirements in 2023","NM cable in insulation requires conduit protection","Only aluminum NM cable is permitted in insulated cavities"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 334.23 updated clearance requirements for NM cable in thermal insulation. When NM cable is surrounded by insulation rather than passing through it, ampacity derating may be required because insulation reduces the cable's ability to dissipate heat."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The 2023 NEC update to MC cable (Article 330) primarily affects:","o":["Minimum conductor size for MC cable","Support requirements for larger trade sizes and installation through framing","Eliminating MC cable use in residential occupancies","Requiring all MC cable to be installed in conduit"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC updates to Article 330 focus on support requirements for larger trade size MC cable and protection requirements when installed through or parallel to framing members — both critical for commercial and industrial MC cable installations."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Which statement about conductor identification is correct under the 2023 NEC?","o":["Any conductor may be re-identified with colored tape regardless of size","Conductors 4 AWG and larger may be re-identified by marking at terminations — conductors smaller than 4 AWG cannot be re-identified","White conductors may never be used as equipment grounding conductors — regardless of size","All conductors must be individually labeled at every junction"],"a":1,"e":"The NEC permits re-identification of conductors 4 AWG and larger (using tape, paint, or other marking) when the conductor is not of an appropriate color for its intended use. Conductors smaller than 4 AWG must be of the correct color and cannot be re-identified."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 300.22, cables installed in air-handling spaces used for environmental air (plenums) must be:","o":["Any cable type if installed in conduit","Listed and marked for use in air-handling spaces (plenum-rated)","Any NM cable with a fire-rated jacket","Metallic conduit only — no cable is permitted in plenums"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 300.22 requires cables installed in air-handling spaces to be listed and marked for use in those spaces (plenum-rated). This prevents toxic gases from being circulated through the HVAC system if the cable burns. Metallic conduit with any cable type is also permitted."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"An electrician finds a junction box that is too small for the number of conductors being installed. The correct action under 2023 NEC 314.16 is:","o":["Add a second junction box in parallel","Replace the box with one that has sufficient cubic inch capacity for all conductors, devices, and fittings","Use a device box and fill calculations are not required","Cap off one conductor to reduce the fill count"],"a":1,"e":"If a box does not have sufficient cubic inch capacity per the 314.16 fill calculation, it must be replaced with an appropriately sized box. Box fill calculations must account for conductors, cable clamps, support fittings, devices, and equipment grounding conductors."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The 2023 NEC update to EMT (Article 358) support requirements affects:","o":["All EMT installations — support intervals were reduced","Support requirements for large trade size EMT in exposed installations","Indoor residential EMT — no longer required for residential work","The maximum trade size of EMT — limited to 2 inches in 2023"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC updates to Article 358.30 address support requirements for large trade size EMT in exposed installations, where the weight and length of the conduit requires more frequent support points than smaller sizes."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Under the 2023 NEC, the ampacity of conductors in a cable tray (Article 392) is governed by:","o":["The cable tray fill percentage only","Section 310.15 ampacity tables, with corrections for the specific installation conditions in 392.22","The temperature rating of the cable tray material","A flat 80% derating regardless of fill"],"a":1,"e":"Article 392.22 references Section 310.15 for conductor ampacity in cable tray installations, with specific application of correction factors based on conductor fill percentage, installation in covered versus uncovered cable tray, and ambient temperature conditions."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 406.12, tamper-resistant receptacles are now required in which new occupancy?","o":["Manufacturing facilities","Child care facilities","Warehouses and storage areas","Agricultural buildings"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 406.12(B)(1) requires tamper-resistant receptacles in child care facilities — a new addition from the 2020 NEC. The expansion also covers healthcare patient areas, hotels/motels, dormitories, and preschool classrooms."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 410.16, which luminaire type is now required in clothes closets?","o":["Any luminaire if properly guarded","Fluorescent only — incandescent is prohibited","LED luminaires — incandescent and fluorescent are no longer permitted","Pendant luminaires rated for damp locations"],"a":2,"e":"2023 NEC 410.16 was completely revised to require LED luminaires in clothes closets. The prior allowances for incandescent and fluorescent sources were removed due to heat and fire hazard concerns when installed near stored clothing."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A homeowner wants to replace a two-prong non-grounding outlet in their 1960s home. Under 2023 NEC 406.4(D), the electrician may:","o":["Install a three-prong receptacle directly — the 1960s wiring is grandfathered","Install a GFCI receptacle with no ground, labeled 'No Equipment Ground'","Install a standard three-prong receptacle only if a new ground wire is run","Replace with the same two-prong type — no upgrade required on replacement"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 406.4(D)(4) permits replacement of non-grounding receptacles with GFCI receptacles when no equipment grounding conductor is available. The GFCI must be labeled 'No Equipment Ground' to inform users and future electricians."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 406.9, what type of cover is now required on ALL wet location receptacles?","o":["Standard flip-up covers — unchanged from 2020","Extra-duty in-use covers — listed covers that close over the plug face while in use","Weatherproof covers with lockable closure","Recessed-in-box receptacles only"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 406.9(B)(1) requires extra-duty in-use covers in all wet locations. Extra-duty covers are listed covers that remain closed over the plug face while the cord is inserted and in use. Standard flip-up covers are no longer acceptable."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 445.18, the disconnecting means for a generator must be located:","o":["At the main electrical panel only","Within sight of the generator","In a locked enclosure at least 10 feet from the generator","At the transfer switch only"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 445.18 requires the generator disconnecting means to be within sight of the generator. This requirement was clarified and strengthened in the 2023 edition, ensuring the disconnect is accessible to a person working at the generator."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A healthcare facility is being renovated. Per 2023 NEC 406.12, the new receptacles in patient care areas must be:","o":["Standard commercial-grade receptacles","Tamper-resistant (TR) receptacles — new 2023 requirement for healthcare patient areas","Hospital-grade receptacles only (not TR)","GFCI-protected only — TR is not required in healthcare"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 406.12(B)(2) requires tamper-resistant receptacles in healthcare occupancy patient care areas — a new requirement added in the 2023 edition. Healthcare receptacles must also meet hospital-grade requirements under other NEC sections."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A variable frequency drive (VFD) is being installed to control a pump motor. Under 2023 NEC 430.227, the new requirement for VFD installations includes:","o":["A dedicated transformer for the VFD","Specific disconnecting means requirements for VFD installations","A minimum 200% overcurrent protection","Grounding through the VFD is not permitted"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 430.227 added new requirements for disconnecting means for variable frequency drives — including provisions for motor-side disconnects and specific requirements for VFD installations that did not exist in prior editions."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"When replacing a receptacle in a bathroom (GFCI-required location) under 2023 NEC 406.4(D), what must be installed?","o":["The same type of receptacle as what was there before — no upgrade required","A GFCI receptacle — the replacement triggers current GFCI requirements for the location","Only a weatherproof receptacle is required in bathrooms","A GFCI circuit breaker must be installed at the panel"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 406.4(D)(1) requires that when a receptacle is replaced in a location that requires GFCI protection, a GFCI receptacle must be installed. There is no grandfather provision — replacement always triggers current requirements."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A generator is installed as a standby power source with a transfer switch. Per 2023 NEC 445.19, what new requirement applies?","o":["The generator must be listed for outdoor use only","The generator must be marked with information about the transfer equipment — new marking requirement","The generator must have a remote shutdown at the main panel","The transfer switch must be located within sight of the generator"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 445.19 added new marking requirements for generators used with transfer equipment. The marking must identify the generator's connection to transfer equipment so that service personnel understand the system configuration."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per the 2023 NEC, pendant luminaires installed in clothes closets are:","o":["Permitted if the wattage is under 60 watts","Permitted if LED type only","Not permitted — regardless of lamp type","Permitted in walk-in closets over 50 sq ft"],"a":2,"e":"Pendant luminaires are not permitted in clothes closets under the 2023 NEC — regardless of lamp type. This prohibition is maintained from prior editions and is based on the proximity of pendant fixtures to stored clothing and the potential for contact and fire."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Under 2023 NEC 450.13, the ventilation clearance requirement for indoor dry-type transformers was updated to:","o":["Require a minimum 18-inch clearance on all sides","Update minimum clearances for modern transformer sizes and configurations","Eliminate ventilation requirements for sealed transformers","Require mechanical ventilation for all transformers over 15 kVA"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 450.13 updated ventilation and clearance requirements for dry-type transformers to reflect modern transformer designs and sizes, ensuring adequate air circulation for heat dissipation without unnecessarily large clearance requirements."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A motel is being built. Per 2023 NEC 406.12, the guest room receptacles must be:","o":["Standard grade — no special requirements for motels","GFCI-protected in bathrooms only","Tamper-resistant — new 2023 requirement for hotel/motel guest rooms","Hospital grade — to withstand heavy use"],"a":2,"e":"2023 NEC 406.12(B)(3) requires tamper-resistant receptacles in hotel and motel guest rooms — a new requirement in the 2023 edition. Hotels and motels are included because children are frequently present as guests."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 430.102(B), a motor controller disconnect may be remotely operated if:","o":["The motor is over 50 horsepower","Specific interlocking conditions are met to ensure the disconnect operates properly","Remote operation is always permitted for motor disconnects","Remote disconnect is only permitted on three-phase motors"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 430.102(B) permits remote operation of motor controller disconnects when specific interlocking conditions are met to ensure the disconnect properly opens and can be verified as open before work begins on the motor or controller."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"When replacing a luminaire in an existing clothes closet, the 2023 NEC 410.16 requires:","o":["No change — existing installation is grandfathered","LED luminaire — the replacement triggers current requirements even in existing closets","The same type as the original luminaire","A written variance from the AHJ before replacement"],"a":1,"e":"When replacing a luminaire in a clothes closet, the replacement triggers the current 2023 NEC requirement for LED luminaires. There is no grandfather provision for luminaire replacements in clothes closets — the new 410.16 requirements apply."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 422.31, a plug-and-cord connected appliance may use the plug as its disconnecting means if the appliance is:","o":["Any portable appliance regardless of rating","Not over 300 VA or 1/8 horsepower for permanently connected appliances","Over 1 horsepower — larger motors require dedicated disconnects","Under 20 amperes and within sight of the appliance"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 422.31 permits a plug-and-cord to serve as the disconnecting means for appliances that are not over 300 VA or 1/8 horsepower. Larger permanently-connected appliances require a dedicated disconnecting means."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Under 2023 NEC 406.12, which elementary school location now requires tamper-resistant receptacles?","o":["Only kindergarten classrooms","All classrooms in preschool and elementary schools — new 2023 requirement","Only classrooms where children under 5 are present","Only art rooms and laboratories"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 406.12(B)(5) requires tamper-resistant receptacles in preschool and elementary school classrooms — a new addition in 2023. This is based on the presence of young children who may be curious about electrical outlets."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A contractor is installing an air-cooled transformer in a commercial building under 2023 NEC 450.9. The primary concern for ventilation is:","o":["The transformer must be enclosed in a fire-rated vault","Adequate air circulation for heat dissipation — minimum clearances must be maintained","Only liquid-cooled transformers require ventilation provisions","Ventilation is only required for transformers over 500 kVA"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 450.9 requires that adequate ventilation be provided for air-cooled transformers to dissipate heat. Minimum clearances around the transformer must be maintained to allow adequate air circulation. The 2023 updates clarified these clearance requirements for modern transformer configurations."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"New Article 626 in the 2023 NEC covers requirements for:","o":["Residential EV charging in attached garages","Electrified truck parking spaces for large commercial vehicles","Solar-powered EV charging stations","All EV charging above 50 amperes"],"a":1,"e":"Article 626 is brand new in the 2023 NEC and specifically covers electrified truck parking spaces — EV charging infrastructure for large commercial trucks, buses, and fleet vehicles in dedicated parking spaces."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 625.48, which new type of EV charging is now addressed?","o":["Level 1 charging for residential use","DC fast charging at commercial stations","Bidirectional charging — EVSE that can supply power from the vehicle back to the building or grid","Wireless EV charging systems"],"a":2,"e":"2023 NEC 625.48 is a new section addressing bidirectional EVSE — equipment that can both charge the vehicle and draw power from the vehicle battery back to the premises (V2H — vehicle to home) or grid (V2G — vehicle to grid)."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"New Article 691 was added to the 2023 NEC to cover:","o":["Residential battery storage systems","Large-scale PV electric supply stations — utility-scale solar","EV fast-charging stations for commercial fleets","Micro-hydro and wind energy systems"],"a":1,"e":"Article 691 covers large-scale PV electric supply stations — utility-scale and large commercial solar installations operated as electric supply stations. This new article fills a gap where the NEC had no specific article for utility-scale solar."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 690.12, rapid shutdown must reduce conductors on a roof to what level within 30 seconds of initiation?","o":["120V or less","50V or less","30V and 240VA or less","0V — complete shutdown required"],"a":2,"e":"2023 NEC 690.12 requires that rapid shutdown reduce conductors within the array and within 3 feet of the roof penetration to 30V and 240VA or less within 30 seconds of initiating the rapid shutdown."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"New 2023 NEC section 706.53 specifically addresses ESS installations in:","o":["Commercial buildings only","Utility-scale battery installations","Dwelling units — a new residential-specific section","Marine and floating building installations"],"a":2,"e":"2023 NEC 706.53 is a new section specifically addressing energy storage system installations in dwelling units, covering permitted locations, prohibited locations (sleeping rooms, bathrooms), ventilation, clearances, and labeling requirements."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 706.53(A)(1), where are energy storage systems (ESS) specifically prohibited in dwelling units?","o":["Garages — too close to vehicles","Sleeping rooms and bathrooms","Basements — moisture concerns","Attics — temperature extremes"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 706.53(A)(1) prohibits ESS installations in sleeping rooms and bathrooms due to fire hazard and gas emission concerns with battery systems. Permitted locations include garages, outside walls, and dedicated mechanical rooms."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The 2023 NEC 625.42 update introduced demand factors for multiple EVSE circuits because:","o":["Multiple chargers always operate at 100% demand","Not all EV chargers will be in use at full load simultaneously — demand credits reduce service size","EV chargers are low-power devices that don't affect load calculations","Demand factors only apply to motors, not EV chargers"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 625.42 introduced demand factors recognizing that when multiple EVSE circuits are installed, they are rarely all operating at full load simultaneously. Demand factors allow designers to size the service and feeder more accurately without over-sizing."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 690.12, rapid shutdown is required for PV systems that are:","o":["Only roof-mounted systems on commercial buildings","All PV systems — ground-mounted and roof-mounted","Building-integrated and building-mounted PV systems","Only systems with microinverters — string inverter systems are exempt"],"a":2,"e":"2023 NEC 690.12 requires rapid shutdown for building-integrated and building-mounted PV systems. Ground-mounted PV systems that are not attached to or on a building are exempt from the rapid shutdown requirement."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 690.41, transformerless PV inverters (ungrounded PV systems) require:","o":["A grounding electrode connected to the negative rail","Special ground-fault protection provisions — specific to ungrounded PV systems","A separately derived system at the inverter output","Grounding of both the positive and negative rails to earth"],"a":1,"e":"Transformerless (ungrounded) PV inverters operate with neither the positive nor negative rail grounded. 2023 NEC 690.41 requires specific ground-fault protection provisions for these systems because they have different fault characteristics than traditional grounded PV systems."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The rapid shutdown initiator for a PV system must be located at:","o":["On the roof adjacent to the PV array","The service equipment or a readily accessible location — 690.12","At the inverter — always within 10 feet of the inverter","At the utility meter only"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 690.12 requires the rapid shutdown initiator to be located at the service equipment or another readily accessible location. This ensures that a first responder arriving at the building can initiate rapid shutdown without accessing the roof."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"New 2023 NEC 706.30 added explicit requirements for:","o":["ESS battery charging rates","Grounding and bonding of energy storage systems","ESS installation in commercial kitchens","ESS interconnection with utility systems"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 706.30 is a new section that added explicit requirements for grounding and bonding of energy storage systems. Prior editions did not have a dedicated grounding section for ESS — installers had to rely on Article 250 cross-references."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 220.57, the new optional calculation method for dwelling units with EV charging allows:","o":["EV charging circuits to be excluded from load calculations","Demand factors for EV circuits to be applied when calculating total dwelling load","EV circuits to be shared with other branch circuits","No change — EV circuits must be added at 100% regardless"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 220.57 provides an optional calculation method for dwelling units with EV charging, allowing demand factors to be applied to EVSE loads when calculating total dwelling load. This helps prevent over-sizing of service equipment for homes with multiple EV charging circuits."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Which of the following statements about large-scale PV installations under 2023 NEC Article 691 is correct?","o":["Article 691 replaces Article 690 for all commercial PV systems","Article 691 applies to large-scale PV operated as an electric supply station — utility-scale systems","Article 691 applies to any rooftop PV system over 100 kW","Article 691 only applies outside the NEC's jurisdiction"],"a":1,"e":"Article 691 applies to large-scale PV systems operated as an electric supply station — essentially utility-scale solar farms. It does not replace Article 690, which still applies to all other PV systems including commercial rooftop installations."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 625.54, EVSE installed in attached garages requires:","o":["No special protection — standard installation","GFCI protection — required for all EVSE in garages","A dedicated subpanel","Exterior weatherproof covers"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 625.54 requires GFCI protection for EVSE installed in garages. This was clarified in the 2023 edition to apply to all EVSE in garage locations, consistent with the GFCI requirements for other receptacles in garage locations."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"The 2023 NEC requires rapid shutdown marking per 690.56(C) to be located:","o":["On the PV inverter only","At a specific location on the building identifying the rapid shutdown initiator and array location","At the utility meter","On each PV module in the array"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 690.56(C) requires marking that identifies the rapid shutdown initiator location and the PV array location on the building. This marking is specifically designed to help first responders quickly locate and initiate rapid shutdown in an emergency."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A residential battery storage system (ESS) is planned for installation in a bedroom. Per 2023 NEC 706.53(A)(1), this installation is:","o":["Permitted if the ESS is in a listed fire-rated enclosure","Permitted if the bedroom is larger than 200 square feet","Prohibited — ESS is not permitted in sleeping rooms","Permitted with written approval from the AHJ"],"a":2,"e":"2023 NEC 706.53(A)(1) explicitly prohibits energy storage systems in sleeping rooms. This prohibition is based on fire hazard and gas emission concerns, particularly with lithium-ion batteries that can off-gas in thermal runaway events."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"New 2023 NEC Article 691 requires that large-scale PV systems be installed and maintained by:","o":["Licensed master electricians only","Licensed journeyman electricians or higher","Qualified persons — with training specific to large-scale PV equipment","Utility company employees only"],"a":2,"e":"2023 NEC 691.6 requires that large-scale PV electric supply stations be installed and maintained by qualified persons — those who have demonstrated skills and knowledge related to the construction and operation of the specific equipment involved in large-scale PV systems."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 590.6(A), GFCI protection for temporary wiring on construction sites applies to:","o":["Only outlets within 10 feet of water","15A and 20A receptacles — GFCI required regardless of location","Only 120V circuits — 240V is exempt","Only when the AHJ requires it"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 590.6(A) requires GFCI protection for all 15A and 20A 125V receptacles used in temporary wiring for construction sites — with no location exceptions. GFCI on construction sites is absolute."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 517.30, hospitals may now use energy storage systems (ESS) for:","o":["Lighting circuits only","The life safety branch of the essential electrical system — with specific requirements","Any branch of the electrical system as a cost-saving measure","Only decorative and non-critical loads"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 517.30 was updated to allow battery energy storage systems to supply the life safety branch of hospital essential electrical systems, provided they meet the 10-second transfer time requirement and other specific conditions."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"All of the following are valid 2023 NEC violations in a new home EXCEPT:","o":["Service installed without a surge protective device","AFCI protection omitted from dormitory branch circuits","LED luminaires installed in a clothes closet","Standard flip-up cover on outdoor GFCI receptacle"],"a":2,"e":"LED luminaires in a clothes closet is NOT a violation — in fact, it is now required per 2023 NEC 410.16. The others are all violations: no SPD violates 230.67, AFCI omitted in dormitory violates 210.12(B), and standard flip-up outdoor cover violates 406.9(B)(1)."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"When replacing service equipment on an existing dwelling unit, the 2023 NEC requires the installation of:","o":["Only what the existing installation had","A listed surge protective device per 230.67(A)","A new grounding electrode if the existing one is over 10 years old","A 200A minimum service regardless of existing loads"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 230.67(A) triggers the SPD requirement when service equipment is replaced — not just on new installations. Replacing a service panel on an existing dwelling requires installation of a listed surge protective device."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 547.10, equipotential planes in agricultural buildings are required primarily to protect:","o":["The electrical wiring from moisture","Livestock from stray voltage — voltage differences between conductive surfaces","The building structure from lightning","Workers from arc flash hazards"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 547.10 requires equipotential planes in livestock confinement areas to protect animals from stray voltage. Livestock are much more sensitive to voltage differences than humans, and even small voltages between water troughs, floor grates, and equipment can cause behavioral and health problems."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A new RV park is being built. Per 2023 NEC Article 551, which new requirement applies to EV charging in the park?","o":["EV charging is not permitted in RV parks","Article 551 was updated to address EV charging provisions for RV parks","RV parks must have DCFC only — no Level 2 permitted","EV charging in RV parks is governed by Article 625 only, not 551"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC Article 551 was updated to address EV charging provisions in RV parks, recognizing that modern RVs increasingly have onboard battery systems and may support EV charging. The update aligns RV park EV provisions with the broader Article 625 requirements."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A field scenario: An electrician replaces a standard receptacle in a hotel guest room without installing a tamper-resistant receptacle. Which 2023 NEC section is violated?","o":["230.67 — surge protection","406.4(D) — replacement requirements, and 406.12(B)(3) — TR required in hotel rooms","410.16 — luminaire requirements","250.94 — intersystem bonding"],"a":1,"e":"Two sections are violated: 406.4(D) which requires that replacements meet current code for the location, and 406.12(B)(3) which requires tamper-resistant receptacles in hotel and motel guest rooms. The replacement triggered compliance with the TR requirement."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"In a new school being built, preschool classrooms require which type of receptacles per 2023 NEC 406.12?","o":["Hospital-grade receptacles","Standard commercial-grade receptacles","Tamper-resistant (TR) receptacles — new 2023 requirement","GFCI receptacles with TR — both required"],"a":2,"e":"2023 NEC 406.12(B)(5) requires tamper-resistant receptacles in preschool and elementary school classrooms — a new addition in 2023. The TR requirement protects young children who may insert objects into receptacles."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 517.16, receptacles in patient care areas of healthcare facilities must now also comply with:","o":["Article 500 hazardous location requirements","Article 406.12 tamper-resistant requirements — cross-reference added in 2023","Article 625 EV charging requirements","Article 706 energy storage requirements"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 517.16 now cross-references 406.12 for tamper-resistant receptacle requirements in patient care areas. This means healthcare patient care receptacles must be both hospital-grade and tamper-resistant."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"A field scenario: A battery ESS is installed in the garage of a dwelling unit. The installation must comply with:","o":["Only Article 250 grounding requirements","2023 NEC 706.53 — which specifically governs ESS in dwelling units","Only Article 706 — no dwelling-specific requirements","Article 230 service equipment requirements only"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 706.53 specifically governs energy storage system installations in dwelling units, including permitted locations (garage is permitted), clearances, ventilation, and labeling. This section was new in 2023 to address the growing number of residential battery storage installations."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"What is the maximum voltage to which conductors on a building-mounted PV system must be reduced within 30 seconds of rapid shutdown initiation?","o":["120V","60V","30V (with 240VA maximum)","0V — complete de-energization"],"a":2,"e":"2023 NEC 690.12 requires that within 30 seconds of rapid shutdown initiation, conductors on the roof and within 3 feet of the roof penetration must be reduced to 30V and 240VA or less. This level is considered safer for first responders working on the roof."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC 230.67(B), service equipment where an SPD is installed must be:","o":["Painted orange to indicate surge protection","Labeled to identify the SPD installation","Inspected annually by the AHJ","Sized at least 20% larger to accommodate the SPD"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 230.67(B) requires that service equipment where an SPD has been installed be labeled to identify the SPD installation. This documentation ensures future electricians and inspectors know an SPD is present."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"An electrician is working on a renovation where a new circuit is being added to an existing panel. The existing panel was installed in 2015. The new circuit must comply with:","o":["The 2011 NEC that was in effect when the panel was installed","The currently adopted NEC in that jurisdiction — typically 2020 or 2023","Only the manufacturer's specifications for the existing panel","No current code — renovations are always exempt"],"a":1,"e":"New circuits added to existing panels must comply with the currently adopted edition of the NEC in that jurisdiction. The 'grandfathering' concept applies to existing installations that remain unchanged — new work triggers current code compliance."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per the 2023 NEC, a new hotel being constructed must have which of the following in each guest room?","o":["GFCI protection only","Tamper-resistant receptacles — 406.12(B)(3)","Hospital-grade receptacles — same as healthcare","Standard grade receptacles — hotels are commercial, not residential"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 406.12(B)(3) requires tamper-resistant receptacles in hotel and motel guest rooms. This is a new requirement in 2023, recognizing that children frequently occupy hotel rooms and are at risk from standard receptacles."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Which of the following scenarios requires an outside emergency disconnect per 2023 NEC 225.41?","o":["A commercial office building","A single-family dwelling unit with a feeder from a separate service","Any structure with a 200A or larger service","Structures with solar installations"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 225.41 requires an outside emergency disconnect for feeders supplying 1- and 2-family dwelling units. This allows first responders to de-energize the dwelling from outside without entering the building."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Which 2023 NEC article specifically addresses safety-related requirements for capacitors?","o":["Article 625","Article 360 — new in 2023","Article 706","Article 691"],"a":1,"e":"Article 360 is a new article added to the 2023 NEC that specifically covers safety-related requirements for capacitors, including energy storage capacitors. Annex R on working with capacitors was also added to support Article 360."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Final scenario: A master electrician is designing a new 4,000 sq ft home with a 400A service, solar PV, a battery ESS, and two EV chargers. Which 2023 NEC sections directly apply to this installation?","o":["Only Article 230 — service equipment","230.67 (SPD), 690.12 (rapid shutdown), 706.53 (ESS in dwelling), 625.42 (EV demand), 220.57 (optional load calc)","Only Article 220 — load calculations","Only Articles 625 and 690 — EV and solar"],"a":1,"e":"A modern home with solar, storage, and EV charging must comply with multiple 2023 NEC sections: 230.67 (SPD at service), 690.12 (rapid shutdown for rooftop PV), 706.53 (ESS location in dwelling), 625.42 (EV demand factors), and 220.57 (optional dwelling load calculation with EV). This is exactly the type of multi-system home being built today."},{"c":"NEC 2023 changes","q":"Per 2023 NEC, all temporary wiring on construction sites must have GFCI protection on 15A and 20A circuits. What is the consequence of not having GFCI on a construction site temporary installation?","o":["No consequence — GFCI is optional on construction sites","Violation of 590.6(A) — an OSHA-enforceable requirement that can result in citation and fine","Only a violation if the AHJ is present","Violation only if an injury occurs"],"a":1,"e":"2023 NEC 590.6(A) requires GFCI on all 15A and 20A temporary wiring receptacles on construction sites. Because OSHA 29 CFR 1926 incorporates NEC requirements by reference, this is both an NEC and OSHA violation — subject to OSHA citation and financial penalties."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"According to DSPS, the 2023 NEC became effective in Wisconsin on:","o":["January 1, 2023","September 1, 2023","September 1, 2026","January 1, 2027"],"a":2,"e":"DSPS adopted the 2023 NEC effective September 1, 2026."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"The 2023 NEC replaces which edition as the Wisconsin base code?","o":["The 2017 NEC","The 2008 NEC","The 2014 NEC","The 2020 NEC"],"a":0,"e":"Wisconsin is moving from the 2017 edition to the 2023 edition."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Because Wisconsin is moving from the 2017 NEC to the 2023 NEC, an electrician should expect:","o":["Changes from the 2023 cycle only","Only editorial changes with no field impact","No changes except Wisconsin amendments","Changes from two revision cycles, 2020 and 2023, at once"],"a":3,"e":"The 2020 edition was not the Wisconsin base edition, so changes first made in 2020 are new to Wisconsin electricians along with the 2023 changes."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"How often does NFPA publish a new edition of the NEC?","o":["Every year","Every three years","Every five years","Only when a state requests it"],"a":1,"e":"The NEC follows a three-year revision cycle, which is why editions are numbered 2017, 2020 and 2023."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Which of the following was NOT one of the three Wisconsin-specific updates highlighted in the DSPS press release?","o":["Whole-home surge protection","A requirement for AFCI protection in all commercial buildings","Restrictions on the number of feeders supplying a building or structure","Permits and inspections for services on vacant land"],"a":1,"e":"The three highlighted items were whole-home surge protection, limits on the number of feeders supplying a building or structure, and permits and inspections for services on vacant land."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Where should you confirm the exact legal wording of a Wisconsin electrical amendment?","o":["A supplier's product catalog","A summary slide in a training course","The NEC Annexes","The current Wisconsin SPS 316 text"],"a":3,"e":"Wisconsin amendments are found in the Wisconsin Administrative Code. Summaries, including training courses, do not replace the adopted text."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Chapters 5, 6 and 7 of the NEC generally:","o":["Supplement or modify the general rules of Chapters 1 through 4 for special occupancies, equipment or conditions","Replace Chapters 1 through 4 entirely","Apply only to utility installations","Apply only to communications circuits"],"a":0,"e":"NEC 90.3 arranges Chapters 5 through 7 as supplements or modifications to the general rules in Chapters 1 through 4."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Chapter 8 of the NEC (communications systems):","o":["Is subject to every rule in Chapters 1 through 7","Applies only to buildings over three stories","Stands alone and is not subject to Chapters 1 through 7 except where specifically referenced","Has been deleted from the 2023 NEC"],"a":2,"e":"NEC 90.3 states that Chapter 8 is not subject to the requirements of Chapters 1 through 7 except where the Chapter 8 rules specifically reference them."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"An Informational Note in the NEC is:","o":["A mandatory rule with penalties","An exception to the rule above it","A Wisconsin amendment","Explanatory material that is not an enforceable requirement"],"a":3,"e":"Informational Notes provide explanation. Enforceable requirements are the mandatory and permissive rules of the code text, as described in NEC 90.5."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"In NEC language, the word shall indicates:","o":["A suggestion only","A mandatory requirement","A permissive option","A manufacturer recommendation"],"a":1,"e":"NEC 90.5 identifies shall and shall not as mandatory rules."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"In NEC language, the phrase shall be permitted indicates:","o":["An action that is always required","An action that is prohibited","An action that is allowed but not required","An action that only inspectors may take"],"a":2,"e":"Permissive rules say what is allowed. Whether to use the option is left to the installer or designer."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Where are terms used in two or more NEC articles generally defined?","o":["Article 100","Article 90","Chapter 9, Table 1","Annex A"],"a":0,"e":"Article 100 contains definitions that apply in two or more articles. Terms used in only one article are defined in that article."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"The Annexes of the NEC are:","o":["Informational and not enforceable unless specifically adopted","Always mandatory","Required reading only for inspectors","Limited to Wisconsin amendments"],"a":0,"e":"Informational annexes are not part of the requirements of the code unless a jurisdiction specifically adopts them."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"In NFPA's marked editions of the NEC, a letter N placed before a section generally indicates:","o":["A note from the NEC committee","A section that applies only to Wisconsin","New material","Material that has been deleted"],"a":2,"e":"The N mark identifies new material. Check your edition's explanation of its change marks."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"The authority having jurisdiction (AHJ) is:","o":["The manufacturer of the equipment","The organization, office or individual responsible for enforcing the code or approving equipment and installations","The NFPA technical committee","The utility lineman on the job"],"a":1,"e":"This is the Article 100 definition of AHJ."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Why does the 2023 NEC have legal force on Wisconsin jobs?","o":["Because NFPA automatically makes it law in every state","Because electricians voluntarily agree to follow it","Because manufacturers require it","Because DSPS adopted it, with any Wisconsin amendments, in the administrative code"],"a":3,"e":"A model code is enforceable only after a jurisdiction adopts it. Wisconsin does so through its administrative code, including SPS 316."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"In addition to adopting the 2023 NEC, what has DSPS said it is doing?","o":["Eliminating the journeyman credential","Removing permit requirements for services","Updating licensing exams","Publishing a separate Wisconsin electrical code in place of the NEC"],"a":2,"e":"DSPS has said it is also updating licensing exams to align with the new code."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"A transient overvoltage is best described as:","o":["A sustained 10 percent voltage rise lasting hours","A complete loss of voltage","A brief drop in voltage below normal","A very short-duration voltage spike above the normal voltage"],"a":3,"e":"Transients last microseconds or less. Sustained overvoltage, sags and outages are different power quality events."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Which of the following can generate a damaging surge inside a building?","o":["Switching of inductive loads such as motors and compressors","Replacing a light bulb","Leaving a door open","Using a battery-powered flashlight"],"a":0,"e":"Both external events like lightning and internal events like switching of inductive loads create transients."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"What is the primary job of an SPD?","o":["Prevent all power outages","Raise voltage during a sag","Limit transient voltage by diverting surge current","Replace the service overcurrent device"],"a":2,"e":"An SPD limits the magnitude of transients. It does not correct sags or outages and does not replace overcurrent protection."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Which component is commonly used in SPDs to clamp voltage?","o":["A fuse link only","A mercury switch","A bimetal strip","A metal oxide varistor (MOV)"],"a":3,"e":"MOVs conduct when voltage rises above a threshold, diverting surge current."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"In the 2023 NEC, overvoltage protection is found in:","o":["Article 90","Article 242","Article 285","Article 310"],"a":1,"e":"The 2023 NEC consolidated overvoltage protection into Article 242."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Which 2017 NEC articles were consolidated into Article 242?","o":["Articles 240 and 250","Articles 210 and 215","Articles 280 and 285","Articles 300 and 310"],"a":2,"e":"Surge arresters (Article 280) and surge-protective devices (Article 285) now appear in Article 242."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"A Type 1 SPD differs from a Type 2 SPD in that a Type 1 device:","o":["May be installed on the line side of the service disconnect overcurrent device","May only be plugged into a receptacle","Is installed only inside appliances","Does not need to be listed"],"a":0,"e":"Type 1 SPDs may be installed on the line side of the service disconnect overcurrent device. Type 2 SPDs are installed on the load side."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"A Type 2 SPD is installed:","o":["On the load side of the service disconnect overcurrent device","Only in the utility transformer","Only at the point of utilization","On the line side of the utility meter"],"a":0,"e":"Type 2 SPDs are permanently connected and installed on the load side of the service disconnect overcurrent device."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"A cord-connected surge strip is generally which SPD type?","o":["Type 1","Type 2","Type 3","Type 4"],"a":2,"e":"Type 3 SPDs are point-of-utilization devices such as cord-connected and receptacle-type devices."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"NEC 230.67 applies to:","o":["Only commercial services","Services supplying dwelling units","Only services over 400 amperes","Only temporary services"],"a":1,"e":"230.67 addresses surge protection for services supplying dwelling units."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Which SPD types may be used to meet NEC 230.67?","o":["Type 3 only","Type 4 only","Any plug-in strip","Type 1 or Type 2"],"a":3,"e":"230.67 requires a Type 1 or Type 2 SPD at the service."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Where must the SPD required by 230.67 generally be located?","o":["Anywhere on the property","Only in the detached garage","As an integral part of the service equipment or immediately adjacent to it","At the last receptacle on each circuit"],"a":2,"e":"The location rule is integral to, or immediately adjacent to, the service equipment, subject to the text's exception."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"When an existing dwelling service equipment is replaced, what does 230.67 require?","o":["Nothing, because the house is existing","Only a plug-in strip at one receptacle","Only a label on the panel","The surge protection requirements apply to the replacement"],"a":3,"e":"230.67 applies when service equipment is replaced. Confirm the adopted wording in the NEC and in SPS 316."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Which of the three Wisconsin-specific updates highlighted by DSPS relates to SPDs?","o":["Permits for vacant land services","Limits on the number of feeders","Whole-home surge protection required","Updated licensing exams"],"a":2,"e":"DSPS highlighted whole-home surge protection."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"An SPD is marked with a short-circuit current rating of 10,000 amperes. Where may it be installed?","o":["Only where the available fault current is 10,000 amperes or less","Anywhere regardless of fault current","Only where fault current is above 10,000 amperes","Only outdoors"],"a":0,"e":"An SPD must not be installed where the available fault current exceeds its marked short-circuit current rating."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"What is the product standard under which SPDs are listed?","o":["UL 943","UL 489","NFPA 70E","UL 1449"],"a":3,"e":"UL 1449 is the standard for surge-protective devices. UL 943 is for GFCIs and UL 489 for molded-case breakers."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"To keep SPD let-through voltage low, connecting conductors should be:","o":["Coiled to add inductance","As short and straight as the manufacturer's instructions allow","As long as possible for flexibility","Spliced in a junction box for easy service"],"a":1,"e":"Long or looped leads add impedance and raise the voltage the protected equipment sees."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"The status indicator on an SPD shows that protection has been lost. What should you do?","o":["Ignore it because the panel still works","Replace the SPD","Cover the indicator","Add a second circuit breaker"],"a":1,"e":"A failed status indicator means the SPD may no longer provide protection and should be replaced."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"The NEC defines a service as:","o":["Any circuit that feeds a motor","The conductors between a panelboard and a receptacle","The conductors and equipment for delivering electric energy from the serving utility to the premises wiring system","Only the utility meter"],"a":2,"e":"This is the Article 100 definition of service."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"A feeder is:","o":["All circuit conductors between the service equipment or other supply source and the final branch-circuit overcurrent device","The conductors between the last overcurrent device and the outlets","The utility conductors at the pole","The grounding electrode conductor"],"a":0,"e":"Feeders are the circuit conductors upstream of the final branch-circuit overcurrent device."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"A branch circuit is:","o":["The circuit conductors between the final overcurrent device and the outlet(s)","All conductors between the service and the first panelboard","The service lateral","A feeder supplying a second building"],"a":0,"e":"This is the Article 100 definition of branch circuit."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"The service point is:","o":["The location of the main bonding jumper","The last receptacle on a circuit","The point of connection between the utility facilities and the premises wiring","The grounding electrode"],"a":2,"e":"The service point is the boundary between the serving utility and the premises wiring."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"The general rule under 230.2 is that a building or structure is supplied by:","o":["Two services","Only one service, unless a permitted condition applies","As many services as the owner wants","One service per floor"],"a":1,"e":"230.2 limits a building or structure to one service, with defined exceptions."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"NEC 230.71 limits the service disconnecting means to not more than:","o":["Two switches or breakers","Ten switches or breakers","No limit","Six switches or six circuit breakers"],"a":3,"e":"230.71 sets a six-disconnect maximum for the service disconnecting means."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"For a one-family dwelling, 230.79 sets the minimum service disconnect rating at:","o":["30 amperes","60 amperes","100 amperes","200 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"230.79 sets a minimum of 100 amperes for a one-family dwelling service disconnect. Verify the adopted text for your job."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"NEC 230.85 applies to:","o":["Industrial motor controllers","Temporary lighting for holidays","Surge arresters over 1000 volts","Emergency disconnects for one- and two-family dwelling units"],"a":3,"e":"230.85 requires an emergency disconnect at one- and two-family dwelling units, readily accessible outdoors."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Under 225.30, a building served by a feeder on the load side of a service disconnect is generally supplied by:","o":["Two feeders","As many feeders as the owner wants","Only one feeder or branch circuit","Three branch circuits"],"a":2,"e":"The basic rule is one supply, unless a recognized condition permits more."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Which of the following highlighted DSPS updates directly concerns feeders?","o":["Restrictions on how many feeders can supply a building or structure","Permits for services on vacant land","Whole-home surge protection","Updated licensing exams"],"a":0,"e":"DSPS highlighted restrictions on the number of feeders supplying a building or structure."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Which condition is recognized by the NEC as a reason for more than one supply to a building?","o":["The owner prefers an extra panel","It saves conductor cost","The new feeder runs in conduit","Fire pumps or emergency systems"],"a":3,"e":"Recognized conditions include fire pumps, emergency systems, legally required standby, optional standby, parallel power production and multiple sources for enhanced reliability."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"The maximum number of disconnects for a building or structure under Article 225 is:","o":["Three","Six","Twelve","There is no maximum"],"a":1,"e":"Article 225 uses a six-disconnect limit similar to services."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"A new feeder to a detached garage in a new installation normally includes:","o":["Ungrounded conductors only","Ungrounded conductors, a grounded conductor, and an equipment grounding conductor","Two ungrounded conductors and nothing else","A grounded conductor bonded to the garage electrode and no EGC"],"a":1,"e":"250.32 calls for an equipment grounding conductor run with the feeder, and the grounded conductor is not bonded to ground at the second building."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"A feeder serves 80 A continuous and 40 A noncontinuous load. The minimum feeder ampacity before adjustments is:","o":["120 A","100 A","160 A","140 A"],"a":3,"e":"(80 x 1.25) + 40 = 140 A."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"DSPS highlighted that which of the following is required for services on vacant land?","o":["Permits and inspections","Nothing, because there is no building","Only a utility sign-off","Only a homeowner affidavit"],"a":0,"e":"DSPS highlighted permits and inspections for services on vacant land."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Temporary wiring for holiday decorative lighting may generally remain in place for no more than:","o":["30 days","180 days","90 days","One year"],"a":2,"e":"590.3 allows temporary installations for holiday decorative lighting and similar purposes for a period not exceeding 90 days."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Where should you check the exact Wisconsin requirements for vacant-land service permits?","o":["A supplier's counter staff","A general NEC training video","The NEC Annexes","The current Wisconsin SPS 316 text and DSPS guidance"],"a":3,"e":"Permitting is a jurisdictional matter, so the answer is in Wisconsin rules and with the AHJ."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"GFCI protection for personnel is designed to trip at ground-fault currents of about:","o":["30 to 60 milliamperes","0.5 to 1 ampere","15 to 20 amperes","4 to 6 milliamperes"],"a":3,"e":"A Class A GFCI trips when ground-fault current is in the range of about 4 to 6 milliamperes."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Which dwelling-unit location is covered by GFCI protection in 210.8?","o":["Hallways","Living rooms","Garages","Closets"],"a":2,"e":"Garages, bathrooms and outdoors are among the locations requiring GFCI protection. Living rooms, hallways and closets are AFCI areas."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"An AFCI is designed to:","o":["Detect ground-fault current of 5 milliamperes","Limit voltage surges","Limit short-circuit current","Recognize an arcing fault signature and open the circuit"],"a":3,"e":"AFCIs address arcing faults, a fire hazard. GFCIs address shock hazards from ground faults."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"AFCI protection in dwelling units applies to which circuits?","o":["Only circuits serving bedrooms","Only outdoor circuits","120-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere branch circuits in kitchens, bedrooms, living areas, hallways, laundry areas and similar rooms","Only circuits over 30 amperes"],"a":2,"e":"210.12 lists many living areas. The scope is not limited to bedrooms."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"AFCI requirements in the NEC have been extended over the revision cycles to include:","o":["Dormitory units and guest rooms and guest suites","Utility transformers","Service entrance conductors","Grounding electrodes"],"a":0,"e":"AFCI scope has expanded to additional occupancies including dormitory units and guest rooms and suites."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"EVSE branch circuits are sized at:","o":["100 percent of the nameplate","80 percent of the nameplate","50 percent of the nameplate","Not less than 125 percent of the maximum EVSE load"],"a":3,"e":"EV charging is a continuous load, so the 125 percent rule applies."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"For service and feeder load calculations, 220.57 sets the EV charging load at:","o":["Always 1500 watts","The larger of 7200 watts or the EVSE nameplate rating","Always the breaker size","50 percent of the vehicle battery capacity"],"a":1,"e":"220.57 uses the larger of 7200 watts or the nameplate rating of the EVSE."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"625.54 requires GFCI protection for personnel for:","o":["Hardwired EVSE disconnecting means only","Receptacles installed for EV charging","Utility meters","Service entrance conductors"],"a":1,"e":"625.54 covers receptacles installed for the connection of EV charging."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Under 625.43, a disconnecting means is required for EVSE rated:","o":["More than 5 amperes","Any rating","Only more than 400 amperes","More than 60 amperes or more than 150 volts to ground"],"a":3,"e":"625.43 requires a disconnecting means for EVSE rated more than 60 A or more than 150 V to ground."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Article 750, added in the 2020 NEC, covers:","o":["Energy management systems","Overvoltage protection","Large-scale PV","Hazardous locations"],"a":0,"e":"Article 750 addresses energy management systems. Overvoltage protection is Article 242."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"The purpose of PV rapid shutdown under 690.12 is to:","o":["Increase PV output","Eliminate the need for grounding","Reduce shock hazard to firefighters by limiting voltage on controlled conductors","Replace the service disconnect"],"a":2,"e":"Rapid shutdown limits voltage on controlled conductors so first responders face less shock hazard."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"The '120 percent rule' in Article 705 relates to:","o":["Maximum voltage drop","Conductor temperature rating","GFCI trip current","The combined overcurrent devices and inverter output compared with the busbar rating"],"a":3,"e":"The 120 percent rule limits load-side connection of sources to busbars relative to their rating."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"Article 706 covers:","o":["Electrified truck parking","Energy storage systems","Surge arresters","Mobile homes"],"a":1,"e":"Article 706 covers energy storage systems, including battery storage."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"The main bonding jumper in a service connects:","o":["The utility transformer to the meter","The EVSE to the vehicle","The grounded conductor and the equipment grounding conductors at the service","The SPD to its breaker"],"a":2,"e":"The main bonding jumper is the connection at the service between the grounded conductor and the equipment grounding conductor or enclosure."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"The intersystem bonding termination in 250.94 provides:","o":["A point for bonding communications systems to the grounding system","A location for the SPD breaker","The disconnect for EVSE","The main bonding jumper"],"a":0,"e":"250.94 provides an accessible termination for bonding communications systems."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"A contractor is replacing the service equipment on a dwelling. Which requirement from this course should be planned for?","o":["A Type 1 or Type 2 SPD under 230.67","A Type 3 plug-in strip","Nothing in Article 230 changed","Removal of the grounding electrode"],"a":0,"e":"230.67 requires Type 1 or Type 2 SPD at dwelling-unit services, and Wisconsin highlighted whole-home surge protection."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"A client wants a service set on vacant land before a house is built. What is the first step?","o":["Install it and ask later","Set it only after the house is complete","Verify permit and inspection requirements with the AHJ and the current SPS 316 text","Skip the grounding electrode until later"],"a":2,"e":"DSPS highlighted permits and inspections for services on vacant land."},{"c":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC","q":"A contractor suggests a second feeder to one detached garage 'for convenience'. What should you do?","o":["Install it, because more feeders are always better","Verify whether a recognized condition under 225.30 and SPS 316 permits it before installing","Install it and label both","Install it only if cheaper"],"a":1,"e":"Convenience is not a recognized condition. DSPS highlighted limits on the number of feeders supplying a building or structure."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under Section 5(a)(1) of the OSH Act (the General Duty Clause), an employer must:","o":["Provide every employee with a written copy of the NEC","Guarantee that no employee will ever be injured on the job","Furnish a place of employment free from recognized hazards that are causing or likely to cause death or serious physical harm","Obtain a state electrical license for each supervisor"],"a":2,"e":"Section 5(a)(1) is the General Duty Clause. It requires employers to furnish a workplace free from recognized hazards causing or likely to cause death or serious physical harm. It does not guarantee zero injuries, and it does not require distributing the NEC."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which OSHA part contains the electrical safety standards for general industry?","o":["29 CFR 1926 Subpart K","29 CFR 1910 Subpart S","29 CFR 1904 Subpart E","29 CFR 1903 Subpart A"],"a":1,"e":"Subpart S of Part 1910 (1910.301–1910.399) is the electrical subpart for general industry. Subpart K of Part 1926 is the electrical subpart for construction."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which OSHA part contains the electrical safety standards for construction?","o":["29 CFR 1910 Subpart S","29 CFR 1904 Subpart C","29 CFR 1977","29 CFR 1926 Subpart K"],"a":3,"e":"29 CFR 1926 Subpart K (1926.400–1926.449) covers electrical requirements in construction, including safety-related work practices such as 1926.416 and the lockout and tagging of circuits in 1926.417."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"What does the OSH Act require of employees under Section 5(b)?","o":["Perform all hazard assessments for their employer","Comply with the standards, rules, regulations and orders that apply to their own actions and conduct","Pay for their own PPE","Report to OSHA before starting any electrical task"],"a":1,"e":"Section 5(b) requires each employee to comply with the standards, rules, regulations and orders applicable to his or her own actions and conduct. It does not make employees responsible for the employer's hazard assessments or PPE costs."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"How does a consensus standard such as NFPA 70E or the NEC generally relate to OSHA enforcement?","o":["It automatically becomes an OSHA regulation when published","It has no relevance to OSHA enforcement under any circumstances","It is not an OSHA regulation unless OSHA incorporates it by reference, but OSHA may treat it as evidence of recognized industry practice","It replaces the OSHA standard whenever the two differ"],"a":2,"e":"Consensus standards are not OSHA regulations unless incorporated by reference. OSHA may still use them as evidence that a hazard is recognized and that feasible abatement methods exist, particularly under the General Duty Clause."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the elements OSHA generally must show to cite the General Duty Clause?","o":["The hazard was recognized","The employer had previously been cited for an unrelated violation","The hazard was causing or likely to cause death or serious physical harm","A feasible and useful method existed to correct the hazard"],"a":1,"e":"The general elements are: a hazard to which employees were exposed, that the hazard was recognized, that it was causing or likely to cause death or serious physical harm, and that a feasible and useful abatement method existed. An unrelated earlier citation is not an element."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"An electrician is servicing wiring in a new building under construction. Which electrical subpart is most directly applicable?","o":["29 CFR 1910 Subpart S only","29 CFR 1904 Subpart B","29 CFR 1903 Subpart B","29 CFR 1926 Subpart K"],"a":3,"e":"Work on a building under construction is construction work, so the construction standards in Part 1926, including Subpart K, apply. If you are uncertain how a particular job is classified, ask a safety professional or OSHA."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.147(a), the lockout/tagout standard states that it does not cover which of the following?","o":["Servicing a machine with stored hydraulic energy","Exposure to electrical hazards from work on, near, or with conductors or equipment in electric utilization installations, which Subpart S covers","Servicing equipment where unexpected start-up could occur","Servicing equipment with an energy-isolating device that can be locked"],"a":1,"e":"1910.147 states that it does not cover exposure to electrical hazards from work on, near, or with conductors or equipment in electric utilization installations; those are covered by Subpart S. It applies to servicing and maintenance of machines and equipment where unexpected energization, start-up or release of stored energy could injure employees."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.132, what is the general rule about who pays for required PPE?","o":["The employer pays for required PPE, with limited exceptions such as ordinary clothing and non-specialty safety-toe footwear","The employee always pays and is reimbursed by the union","The employee pays for any PPE bought before being hired","Employers never have to pay for any PPE"],"a":0,"e":"OSHA's PPE payment requirement in 1910.132(h) generally requires employers to pay for PPE they require, with limited exceptions, including everyday clothing and non-specialty safety-toe footwear that may be worn off the job."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which statement about the right to refuse dangerous work is most accurate?","o":["Employees may refuse any task they dislike without consequence","Employees may only refuse work after OSHA has issued a citation","The right is narrow and generally applies only to an imminent danger of death or serious injury, where there is no time to get it corrected through regular channels","Employees have no right to refuse work under any circumstances"],"a":2,"e":"OSHA's regulation at 29 CFR 1977.12 describes narrow conditions, generally an imminent danger of death or serious injury that a reasonable person would recognize, with insufficient time to resolve it through regular channels, and, where possible, a request that the employer correct it."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"What does Section 11(c) of the OSH Act protect?","o":["Employers from penalties if they self-report a violation","Employees from all discipline for any workplace reason","Contractors from having to pay for PPE","Employees from retaliation for exercising rights under the Act, such as reporting a hazard or injury"],"a":3,"e":"Section 11(c) prohibits retaliation against employees for exercising their rights under the OSH Act. It does not shield employees from all discipline for unrelated reasons."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.399, a 'qualified person' is best described as:","o":["One familiar with the construction and operation of the equipment and the hazards involved","Any person who holds a state electrician license","Any person with five years of experience in any trade","Any person supervised by a master electrician"],"a":0,"e":"1910.399 defines a qualified person as one familiar with the construction and operation of the equipment and the hazards involved. Qualification depends on the equipment and circumstances, so a person can be qualified for some equipment and unqualified for other equipment."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"An electrician is fully qualified on 120/208-volt panelboards. Based on OSHA's note to the qualified-person definition, what can be concluded about unfamiliar medium-voltage equipment?","o":["The electrician is not automatically qualified for it; qualification depends on familiarity with that equipment and its hazards","The electrician is automatically qualified for all electrical equipment","The electrician is qualified if the equipment is rated under 1000 volts only","The electrician is qualified as long as a license is current"],"a":0,"e":"OSHA's note to the definition explains that a person may be qualified for certain equipment and unqualified for other equipment. Qualification is specific to the equipment and hazards involved."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"At a minimum, which topic must qualified persons be trained in under 1910.332?","o":["How to prepare the OSHA 300 log","Skills and techniques to distinguish exposed live parts from other parts of electrical equipment and to determine the nominal voltage of exposed live parts","Procedures for filing OSHA citations","Contract negotiation with utilities"],"a":1,"e":"1910.332 requires qualified persons to be trained in, at a minimum, distinguishing exposed live parts from other parts, determining the nominal voltage of exposed live parts, and the clearance distances in 1910.333(c) with the corresponding voltages."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 29 CFR 1926.32(f), a 'competent person' is one who:","o":["Holds a master electrician license","Has completed a four-year engineering degree","Can identify existing and predictable hazards and has authorization to take prompt corrective measures to eliminate them","Is employed by OSHA"],"a":2,"e":"The construction standards define a competent person as one capable of identifying existing and predictable hazardous or dangerous conditions and who has authorization to take prompt corrective measures. The term depends on capability and authority, not on a particular license or degree."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which statement about electric shock injury is most accurate?","o":["The amount of current through the body, its path and its duration are what determine the injury","Only voltages above 600 volts can seriously injure a person","Injury depends only on the size of the person","Current does not matter; only the voltage rating matters"],"a":0,"e":"Injury severity depends on current magnitude, the path through the body and the duration of flow. Voltage matters because it drives current, but serious or fatal injury can occur at common utilization voltages."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 29 CFR 1904.39, an employer must report a work-related fatality to OSHA within:","o":["8 hours","24 hours","48 hours","7 days"],"a":0,"e":"All covered employers must report a work-related fatality within 8 hours. In-patient hospitalizations, amputations and losses of an eye must be reported within 24 hours."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 29 CFR 1904.39, an employer must report a work-related in-patient hospitalization of an employee to OSHA within:","o":["8 hours","72 hours","30 days","24 hours"],"a":3,"e":"A work-related in-patient hospitalization, amputation or loss of an eye must be reported within 24 hours of the employer learning of it. A fatality must be reported within 8 hours."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which standard states that it does not cover exposure to electrical hazards from work on, near, or with conductors or equipment in electric utilization installations?","o":["29 CFR 1926.417, because Subpart K excludes them","29 CFR 1910.147, because those hazards are covered by Subpart S","29 CFR 1904.39, because it covers only recordkeeping","29 CFR 1910.132, because it governs only eye protection"],"a":1,"e":"1910.147(a) states that the standard does not cover exposure to electrical hazards from work on, near or with conductors or equipment in electric utilization installations; those are covered by Subpart S, including 1910.333."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.333(a)(1), live parts to which an employee may be exposed must be de-energized before the employee works on or near them unless:","o":["The employer can demonstrate that de-energizing introduces additional or increased hazards or is infeasible due to equipment design or operational limitations","The employee has a state electrician license","The task will take less than an hour","The equipment is operating at less than 20 amperes"],"a":0,"e":"The rule is to de-energize unless the employer can demonstrate that doing so introduces additional or increased hazards or is infeasible. Examples include shutting down life support systems or testing circuits that can only be tested while energized."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"What are the three required components of an energy control program under 1910.147(c)?","o":["Energy control procedures, employee training, and periodic inspections","Lockout locks, tag printing, and storage cabinets","Hazard assessment, medical surveillance, and air monitoring","Permits, insurance, and union approval"],"a":0,"e":"1910.147(c) requires the employer to establish a program consisting of energy control procedures, employee training and periodic inspections."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"The employer must conduct a periodic inspection of each energy control procedure at least:","o":["Annually","Every five years","Only after an injury","Every two years"],"a":0,"e":"1910.147(c)(6) requires periodic inspection of the energy control procedure at least annually, performed by an authorized employee other than the one(s) using the procedure being inspected."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.147, an authorized employee is:","o":["A person who locks out or tags out machines or equipment in order to perform servicing or maintenance","A person who only operates the machine during normal production","A person whose workstation is in a different building","Any supervisor on site regardless of training"],"a":0,"e":"An authorized employee locks out or tags out equipment to perform servicing or maintenance. An affected employee is one who operates the equipment or works in an area where servicing is performed."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"If an energy-isolating device is capable of being locked out, what does 1910.147 generally require the employer's program to use?","o":["Tagout only, because tags are easier to use","Lockout, unless the employer can demonstrate that tagout will provide full employee protection","Either, at the preference of the nearest supervisor","Neither; only a written warning at the panel is required"],"a":1,"e":"If an energy-isolating device can be locked out, the program must use lockout unless the employer demonstrates that a tagout system will provide full employee protection."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.147(c)(5), which is a required characteristic of lockout and tagout devices?","o":["They are red, rectangular and no longer than 3 inches","They are durable, standardized, substantial, and identify the employee who applied them","They are interchangeable among all workers","They are used for other purposes when not locked out"],"a":1,"e":"Devices must be durable, standardized by color, shape or size, substantial enough to prevent inadvertent removal, and identifiable as to the individual who applied them. They must be used only for energy control."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"What minimum unlocking strength does 1910.147 specify for a tagout device attachment means?","o":["No less than 50 pounds","No less than 5 pounds","No less than 500 pounds","No requirement for strength is stated"],"a":0,"e":"Tagout device attachment means must be non-reusable, attachable by hand, self-locking and non-releasable with a minimum unlocking strength of no less than 50 pounds."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.333(b)(2), when may a tag be used on a disconnecting means without a lock?","o":["Whenever the job is expected to take less than one shift","If a lock cannot be applied, or tagging is demonstrated to provide equivalent safety, and at least one additional equivalent safety measure is also used","Whenever only one person is working on the equipment","Whenever the equipment is operated at less than 120 volts"],"a":1,"e":"A tag may be used without a lock if a lock cannot be applied or tagging provides equivalent safety, but it must be supplemented by at least one additional safety measure providing equivalent protection, such as removing an isolating circuit element."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which list shows the correct order of the 1910.147(d) steps?","o":["Verification, preparation, device application, shutdown, isolation, stored energy","Device application, shutdown, preparation, verification, isolation, stored energy","Preparation, shutdown, isolation, device application, control of stored energy, verification","Shutdown, verification, isolation, preparation, stored energy, device application"],"a":2,"e":"1910.147(d) sets out preparation for shutdown, machine or equipment shutdown, isolation, lockout/tagout device application, control of stored or residual energy, and verification of isolation, in that order."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.333(b)(2)(iv), who must verify that equipment cannot be restarted and test for de-energization?","o":["Any employee in the work area","The OSHA compliance officer","A qualified person","The equipment manufacturer's sales representative"],"a":2,"e":"A qualified person must operate the equipment operating controls or otherwise verify it cannot be restarted, and must use test equipment to verify the circuit elements and parts are de-energized."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"The test for de-energization under 1910.333(b)(2)(iv) must also determine whether any energized condition exists because of:","o":["Only the position of the disconnect handle","Weather conditions at the site","Inadvertently induced voltage or unrelated voltage backfeed","The age of the circuit breaker"],"a":2,"e":"The test must determine if any energized condition exists as a result of inadvertently induced voltage or unrelated voltage backfeed, even though specific parts of the circuit have been de-energized and presumed safe."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"A control circuit device such as a push button, selector switch or interlock may be used as:","o":["The sole means of de-energizing if the switch is labeled","A substitute for lockout if the interlock is tested monthly","Not the sole means of de-energizing circuits or equipment, and not a substitute for lockout and tagging procedures","A valid isolating device under all conditions"],"a":2,"e":"Under 1910.333(b)(2)(ii), control circuit devices such as push buttons, selector switches and interlocks may not be used as the sole means for de-energizing circuits or equipment, and interlocks are not a substitute for lockout and tagging."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under group lockout (1910.147(f)(3)), what must each authorized employee do?","o":["Rely on the group leader's lock alone","Remove all locks at the end of the shift regardless of who applied them","Leave the group device unlocked until the job is complete","Affix a personal lockout or tagout device to the group lockout device, lockbox or comparable mechanism when beginning work"],"a":3,"e":"Each authorized employee affixes a personal lockout or tagout device to the group mechanism when beginning work and removes it when he or she stops working on the equipment."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.333(b)(2)(v), which action must come last in the sequence before circuits or equipment are re-energized?","o":["A test of tools to verify jumpers and grounds have been removed","A warning to exposed employees to stay clear","A decision about whether to apply new tags","A visual determination that all employees are clear of the circuits and equipment"],"a":3,"e":"The sequence is: tests and visual inspections by a qualified person that tools, jumpers, shorts and grounds are removed; warn exposed employees; remove locks and tags properly; and then visually determine that all employees are clear."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Who is generally permitted to remove a lock or tag under 1910.333(b)(2)(v)?","o":["Any employee who needs the equipment to run","The first supervisor to arrive on the next shift","Any employee with a bolt cutter","The employee who applied it or someone under that employee's direct supervision, with a narrow exception when that employee is absent"],"a":3,"e":"Each lock and tag is removed by the employee who applied it or under direct supervision. If that employee is absent, a designated qualified person may remove it only if the employer ensures the applying employee is unavailable and that he or she knows of the removal before resuming work."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"After a circuit breaker trips on a feeder, a worker wants to reset it repeatedly until it holds. What does 1910.334(b)(2) say?","o":["Reclose it three times before investigating","Reclose it only if no one is within 10 feet of the panel","Reclosing is encouraged to locate the fault","Repetitive manual reclosing is prohibited; the circuit and equipment must be determined safe to energize before re-energizing"],"a":3,"e":"1910.334(b)(2) prohibits repetitive manual reclosing of circuit breakers and re-energizing circuits through replaced fuses. The circuit may not be manually re-energized until it has been determined that the equipment and circuit can be safely energized."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which of the following is an example given by OSHA of de-energizing introducing an additional or increased hazard?","o":["Working in a space that is cold","Rescheduling work to the next day","Interruption of life support equipment","A supervisor preferring to avoid a shutdown"],"a":2,"e":"OSHA's examples of increased or additional hazards include interruption of life support equipment, deactivation of emergency alarm systems, shutdown of hazardous location ventilation equipment, and removal of illumination for an area. Convenience or schedule is not an exception."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.333(c), who may work on electric circuit parts or equipment that have not been de-energized under the lockout and tagging procedures?","o":["Any employee with a hard hat and gloves","Only qualified persons","Any employee supervised by an apprentice","Any person with a valid driver's license"],"a":1,"e":"Only qualified persons may work on electric circuit parts or equipment that have not been de-energized under the lockout and tagging procedures, and they must be capable of working safely on energized circuits and familiar with the special precautionary techniques, PPE, insulating materials and insulated tools."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"If signs and barricades do not provide necessary protection on or adjacent to electrical equipment or lines, OSHA requires:","o":["An attendant to be stationed to warn and protect employees","The area to be left unmarked","A second sign to be added only","The equipment to be painted a bright color"],"a":0,"e":"1910.335(b) provides that if signs and barricades do not provide the necessary protection, an attendant must be stationed to warn and protect employees."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.333(c)(3), an unqualified person working near unguarded, energized overhead lines of 50 kV or below must keep themselves and the longest conductive object they may contact at least:","o":["10 feet away","3 feet away","25 feet away","1 foot away"],"a":0,"e":"For voltages to ground of 50 kV or below, the standard's distance for unqualified persons is 10 feet. For higher voltages the distance increases. Qualified line workers and crane operations follow other, more detailed standards."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"For unguarded energized overhead lines over 50 kV, the distance for unqualified persons in 1910.333(c)(3) is:","o":["10 feet regardless of voltage","10 feet plus 4 inches for every 10 kV over 50 kV","The same distance as for 50 kV or less, divided by two","Whatever distance the foreman chooses"],"a":1,"e":"For voltages over 50 kV the minimum distance is 10 feet plus 4 inches for every 10 kV over 50 kV. This general rule does not replace the specific requirements for line workers and for cranes and derricks in construction."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Before work begins in construction, 1926.416(a)(2) requires the employer to:","o":["Assume no energized circuits exist near the site","Ask the lowest-paid employee to look for lines","Ascertain by inquiry, direct observation or instruments whether any part of an energized power circuit is located where the work may result in contact","Wait until a contact occurs and then call the utility"],"a":2,"e":"1926.416(a)(2) requires the employer to ascertain, by inquiry, direct observation or instruments, whether any part of an energized electric power circuit, exposed or concealed, is located so that work may bring a person, tool or machine into contact."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"A boom truck contacts an overhead line, and nobody is in danger from fire. What is the safest action for the operator?","o":["Stay in the cab, warn others to stay away, and wait for the utility to confirm the line is de-energized","Climb out and inspect the damage immediately","Step out and touch the truck to check whether it is energized","Order a coworker to pull the boom away by hand"],"a":0,"e":"The operator should stay in the cab, warn others to keep away and have the utility de-energize the line. Anyone touching the equipment and the ground at the same time can become the path for current."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"If an operator must leave equipment that contacted a power line because of a fire, how should they exit?","o":["Climb down while holding the handrail","Step out and walk away with long strides","Run toward the fence line","Jump clear without touching the equipment and ground at the same time, land with feet together, and shuffle or hop away"],"a":3,"e":"The operator should jump clear so that no part of the body touches the equipment and the ground at once, land with feet together and shuffle or hop away to limit the voltage difference between feet (step potential)."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"A GFCI designed for personnel protection is designed to trip when the ground-fault current is about:","o":["500 milliamperes","15 amperes","100 amperes","5 milliamperes (within a range of about 4 to 6 milliamperes)"],"a":3,"e":"Personnel-protection GFCIs trip at ground-fault current in the range of about 4 to 6 milliamperes. They are not overcurrent devices, so breakers or fuses are still required."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"A worker standing on dry ground touches both the hot and neutral conductors on the load side of a GFCI. What is true?","o":["The GFCI will always trip faster than a breaker","The GFCI will always prevent injury","The GFCI may not trip, because current flowing from hot to neutral through the person is not a ground-fault imbalance","The GFCI cannot be used in that circuit"],"a":2,"e":"A GFCI detects current leaking to ground. If current flows from hot to neutral through a person without leaking to ground, the device sees no imbalance and may not trip. GFCIs reduce shock risk, but they do not replace safe work practices."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"On a construction site, which receptacle outlets must have GFCI protection for personnel under 1926.404(b)(1)?","o":["Only outlets located above the second floor","Only outlets used for lighting","Only outlets on circuits above 480 volts","120-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere outlets that are not part of the permanent wiring and are in use by employees"],"a":3,"e":"1926.404(b)(1) requires approved GFCIs on all 120-volt, single-phase 15- and 20-ampere receptacle outlets on construction sites that are not part of the permanent wiring of the building or structure and are in use by employees."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which is a required element of the assured equipment grounding conductor program under 1926.404(b)(1)?","o":["Annual replacement of every extension cord","A written program description and one or more designated competent persons to implement the program","A requirement that all tools be battery-powered","Approval of the program by the local utility"],"a":1,"e":"The program must be described in writing and available at the job site, and the employer must designate one or more competent persons to implement it. It also requires inspections, testing and records."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under the assured equipment grounding conductor program, cord sets and equipment connected by cord and plug must be visually inspected:","o":["Only once at purchase","Before each day's use (except items that are fixed and not exposed to damage)","Only after an injury","Once per calendar year"],"a":1,"e":"The standard requires visual inspection for external defects before each day's use, with an exception for cord sets and receptacles that are fixed and not exposed to damage. Required tests are performed at specified times and intervals."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"A portable cord-and-plug tool has a pinched, crushed outer jacket on its cord. What does 1910.334(a)(2) require?","o":["Wrap the damaged area with tape and continue using it","Use it only for outdoor work","Remove the item from service and do not use it until repairs and tests necessary to make it safe have been made","Use it only if the housing is dry"],"a":2,"e":"If there is a defect or evidence of damage that might expose an employee to injury, the item must be removed from service. It cannot be used until repairs and tests necessary to render it safe have been made."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.334(a)(3), adapters that interrupt the continuity of the equipment grounding connection:","o":["May be used if the tool is double insulated and the worker is dry","May not be used","May be used if the cord is under 25 feet long","May be used when plugging into a two-slot outlet"],"a":1,"e":"Attachment plugs and receptacles may not be connected or altered in a manner that would interrupt the continuity of the equipment grounding circuit, and adapters that interrupt that continuity may not be used."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under the construction standards, temporary lights:","o":["May always be hung by their cords for convenience","May be suspended by their cords if the cord is taped","May be hung by the cord only outdoors","May not be suspended by their electric cords unless the cords and lights are designed for this means of suspension"],"a":3,"e":"1926.405(a)(2)(ii) provides that temporary lights may not be suspended by their electric cords unless the cords and lights are designed for suspension."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.333(c)(7), portable ladders used where the employee or ladder could contact exposed energized parts must have:","o":["Nonconductive siderails","Metal siderails for strength","Rubber feet only","A bright color"],"a":0,"e":"Portable ladders must have nonconductive siderails if they are used where the employee or the ladder could contact exposed energized parts."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Portable electric lighting used in wet or other conductive locations in construction, such as inside a tank, must operate at:","o":["12 volts or less, or 120 volts if protected by a GFCI","240 volts if the cord is yellow","277 volts with a double-pole breaker","Any voltage if the worker wears boots"],"a":0,"e":"1926.405(a)(2)(ii) requires such lighting to be operated at 12 volts or less, though 120-volt lights may be used if protected by a ground-fault circuit interrupter."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"In the hierarchy of controls, which approach is placed first for electrical hazards?","o":["Wearing the highest category of PPE","Elimination: de-energizing, locking out and verifying","Posting a warning sign","Scheduling the work for a quiet time"],"a":1,"e":"Elimination is first. For electrical hazards that means de-energizing, locking out and verifying. PPE is the last line of defense."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1910.132(d), after the employer assesses the workplace for hazards requiring PPE, the employer must:","o":["File the assessment with the state licensing board","Have each employee sign a waiver","Verify the assessment with a written certification that identifies the workplace evaluated, the person certifying and the date","Destroy the assessment after one year"],"a":2,"e":"1910.132(d)(2) requires the employer to verify that the workplace hazard assessment has been performed through a written certification that identifies the workplace evaluated, the person certifying that the evaluation has been performed, and the date of the hazard assessment."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"What must be done to rubber insulating gloves before each day's use, per 1910.137?","o":["Wash them with solvent","Mark the date on the cuff and store them","Inspect them for damage and give them an air test","Stretch them to the maximum to check elasticity"],"a":2,"e":"Rubber insulating equipment must be inspected for damage before each day's use and immediately following any incident that can reasonably be suspected of causing damage. Insulating gloves must also be given an air test along with the inspection."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Protector gloves, normally leather, are worn:","o":["Instead of rubber insulating gloves","Under rubber insulating gloves","Over rubber insulating gloves, with limited exceptions","Only when the gloves are wet"],"a":2,"e":"Protector gloves are worn over insulating gloves to protect the rubber from cuts and punctures. 1910.137 provides limited exceptions, such as for Class 0 gloves or where high finger dexterity is needed for small parts."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"In construction, an employee on an unprotected side or edge must generally be protected from falling at what height above a lower level?","o":["2 feet or more","6 feet or more","15 feet or more","30 feet or more"],"a":1,"e":"1926.501(b)(1) requires protection from falling for each employee on a walking or working surface with an unprotected side or edge that is 6 feet or more above a lower level. For general industry the trigger is 4 feet."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"What angle rule applies to setting a non-self-supporting ladder, such as an extension ladder?","o":["The ladder should be set as close to vertical as possible","The horizontal distance from the top support to the foot of the ladder is about one-quarter of the working length of the ladder","The horizontal distance equals half the working length","The ladder should be leaned at about 45 degrees"],"a":1,"e":"Non-self-supporting ladders must be used at an angle such that the horizontal distance from the top support to the foot is approximately one-quarter of the ladder's working length (the 4-to-1 rule)."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"A portable ladder is used to reach an upper landing. How far must the side rails extend above the landing, unless the ladder is secured and an alternative grab-rail is provided?","o":["At least 6 inches","At least 3 feet","At least 8 feet","They do not need to extend at all"],"a":1,"e":"When portable ladders are used for access to an upper landing surface, the side rails must extend at least 3 feet above the landing, or the ladder must be secured at its top and a grasping device provided."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"On a stepladder, the top or top step:","o":["May not be used as a step","May be used if the ladder is Type I","May be used if the worker is holding a tool","May be used only outdoors"],"a":0,"e":"The top or top step of a stepladder may not be used as a step. Standing on the top cap or top step reduces stability and removes a handhold."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Under 1926.502(d), an anchorage for a personal fall arrest system must support at least how many pounds per attached employee, or be designed by a qualified person with a safety factor of at least two?","o":["500 pounds","1,000 pounds","5,000 pounds","50 pounds"],"a":2,"e":"Anchorages used for personal fall arrest equipment must be capable of supporting at least 5,000 pounds per employee attached, or be designed, installed and used as part of a complete system with a safety factor of at least two, under the supervision of a qualified person."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"A co-worker is still gripping an energized conductor and cannot let go. What should you do first?","o":["Pull the person free with your bare hands","Pour water on the person to cool the burns","Wait several minutes to see whether the person releases","Do not touch the person; shut off the power if you can safely do so and call for emergency help"],"a":3,"e":"Touching the victim may allow current to pass to you. De-energize if it can be done safely, call emergency services, and then give care when the scene is safe."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which statement about fires on energized electrical equipment is correct?","o":["They are Class C fires; de-energize if safe, and do not use water on the energized equipment","Water is the preferred agent for all electrical fires","They are Class D fires that can only be fought by a utility","They cannot be extinguished until the building is evacuated by the fire department"],"a":0,"e":"Fires involving energized electrical equipment are Class C. If safe, de-energize the equipment, and use an extinguisher rated for the hazard. Water must not be used on energized equipment."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"A worker receives a shock and says they feel fine. What is the best course of action?","o":["Return to work and monitor the symptoms","Arrange medical evaluation, because internal injuries and heart rhythm problems may be delayed or hidden","Take a break for an hour and resume work","No evaluation is needed unless there is a visible burn"],"a":1,"e":"Every shock victim should have a medical evaluation even if they seem fine. The current can cause internal injuries and heart rhythm disturbances that may not be immediately apparent."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which step of the 1910.147(d) procedure comes immediately after relieving, disconnecting, restraining or otherwise rendering safe the stored or residual energy?","o":["Verification of isolation","Preparation for shutdown","Application of the lockout device","Machine or equipment shutdown"],"a":0,"e":"The sequence is preparation, shutdown, isolation, device application, control of stored or residual energy, and then verification of isolation before work begins."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"A crew must place a ladder near overhead lines of 12 kV on a site. What is the correct approach?","o":["Have the lines de-energized and grounded or otherwise protected, or position the work so neither person nor ladder can come within the required distance, and do not use a conductive ladder","Use an aluminum ladder because it is lighter to reposition","Work within reach of the conductors if the ladder is dry","Proceed if the lines appear to be insulated"],"a":0,"e":"Treat overhead lines as energized and uninsulated. Arrange for de-energizing and grounding or other protective measures, keep persons and conductive objects outside the required distances, and avoid conductive ladders where contact is possible."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which of the following events carries a 24-hour (rather than 8-hour) reporting deadline to OSHA under 1904.39?","o":["A work-related fatality","A first aid treatment of a minor cut","An employee being late to work","A work-related in-patient hospitalization of an employee"],"a":3,"e":"In-patient hospitalizations, amputations and losses of an eye must be reported within 24 hours. A fatality must be reported within 8 hours. First aid treatment alone does not trigger a report."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"Which statement is correct about when a lockout device applied by an authorized employee may be removed by someone else under 1910.147(e)(3)?","o":["Any supervisor may cut the lock at any time","Any co-worker may remove it if the job is urgent","The lock may be removed by anyone at the end of the shift","Only through a documented procedure that the employer demonstrates is as safe, including verifying the employee is not at the facility and making sure the employee knows before resuming work"],"a":3,"e":"The general rule is that the device is removed by the employee who applied it. The narrow exception requires a specific, documented procedure with at least verification that the employee is not at the facility, reasonable efforts to contact them, and ensuring they know before resuming work."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"A technician is servicing a machine whose motor is fed by a branch circuit. Which statement best describes how the standards fit together?","o":["Neither standard applies to any electrician","Only 1926.417 applies in general industry","1910.147 applies to all work on building wiring and Subpart S never applies","1910.147 applies to servicing the machine; Subpart S (1910.333) governs the electrical circuits and conductors, and employers often combine both into a single program"],"a":3,"e":"1910.147 covers servicing and maintenance of machines and equipment, while exposure to electrical hazards from work on, near or with conductors or equipment in electric utilization installations is covered by Subpart S, including 1910.333. Many employers write one program that satisfies both."},{"c":"Electrical safety & OSHA","q":"A GFCI protecting a temporary 120-volt receptacle on a construction site repeatedly trips when a particular saw is plugged in. What should be done?","o":["Remove the saw and its cord from service, inspect and test them, and repair or replace them; do not bypass the GFCI","Plug the saw into an outlet without GFCI protection","Defeat the GFCI so the saw can run","Tape the reset button down"],"a":0,"e":"A GFCI that trips is signaling a ground fault, in this case probably in the saw or its cord. The tool and cord must be removed from service until repaired and tested. The GFCI must never be bypassed or defeated."}];